∇Z←dm∆SplineSolver
⍝ Linear-system method used by the spline least-squares fits.
⍝
⍝ Every fit forms symmetric normal equations (B^T B + P) c = B^T y. Cholesky is exact but
⍝ costs O(K^3) for K unknowns; conjugate gradient on the sparse system costs roughly
⍝ O(nnz) per iteration and suits large, well-conditioned (usually penalized) systems.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ dm::fda::SplineSolveOptions options{dm::fda::SplineSolver::ConjugateGradient};
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:** `Z←dm∆SplineSolver` (a 2-column matrix: names, values). Each enumerator is a niladic function:
⍝ - `dm∆SplineSolver∆Automatic` = 0
⍝ - `dm∆SplineSolver∆Cholesky` = 1
⍝ - `dm∆SplineSolver∆ConjugateGradient` = 2
⍝
⍝ Enumerators are numbers; functions also accept their names as character vectors.
⍝
⍝ - C++: `enum class datamunge::fda::SplineSolver`
 Z←3 2⍴(,'Automatic') 0 (,'Cholesky') 1 (,'ConjugateGradient') 2
∇

∇Z←dm∆SplineSolver∆Automatic
⍝ Enumerator `datamunge::fda::SplineSolver::Automatic` = 0.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:** `Z←dm∆SplineSolver∆Automatic`
 Z←0
∇

