∇Z←dm∆Alternative
⍝ Prespecified tail of a test's null reference, like R's alternative argument. Location/proportion alternatives refer to the signed effect; KS alternatives refer to empirical CDF ordering and consequently reverse the stochastic-location direction.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto a=dm::stats::Alternative::Less;
⍝ const auto t=dm::stats::t_test_one_sample({1,2,3,4},0,a);
⍝ std::print("{}\n",t.alternative==a); // true
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/t.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:** `Z←dm∆Alternative` (a 2-column matrix: names, values). Each enumerator is a niladic function:
⍝ - `dm∆Alternative∆TwoSided` = 0
⍝ - `dm∆Alternative∆Less` = 1
⍝ - `dm∆Alternative∆Greater` = 2
⍝
⍝ Enumerators are numbers; functions also accept their names as character vectors.
⍝
⍝ - C++: `enum class datamunge::stats::Alternative`
