∇Z←H dm∆RPlot∆show_grid B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Turns the light background grid on or off (on by default), like adding or omitting R's `grid()`.
⍝
⍝ One vertical line per x tick and one horizontal line per y tick, so the grid always lines
⍝ up with the tick labels (round values, evenly spaced ticks, or custom tick positions).
⍝ Hidden axes hide the grid too.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ auto plt = dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0, 2.0), dm::c(1.0, 2.0)).show_grid(false);
⍝ std::print("{}\n", plt.grid_visible());  // false
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - R `grid()`: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/grid.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆show_grid ⍬
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆show_grid enabled
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `enabled` boolean (0 or 1), default 1: True (the default) draws the grid; false omits it.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RPlot handle: This plot, for chaining.
⍝
⍝ - C++: `RPlot & datamunge::graphics::RPlot::show_grid(bool enabled = true)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆grid_color()`, `dm∆hide_axes()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1035 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆RPlot∆x_limits B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Fixes the visible x range, like R's `xlim = c(min_x, max_x)`.
⍝
⍝ Without limits the x range is fitted to every layer (and vertical reference line), plus
⍝ 5% padding on each side. With limits, the plotting area spans exactly [`min_x`, `max_x`]
⍝ with no padding (R pads `xlim` by 4% unless `xaxs = "i"`). Data outside the range is not
⍝ clipped and may be drawn over the margins.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ auto plt = dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0, 2.0, 3.0), dm::c(1.0, 4.0, 9.0))
⍝                .x_limits(0.0, 4.0)
⍝                .y_limits(0.0, 10.0);
⍝ std::print("{} {} {}\n", plt.has_x_limits(), plt.x_max(), plt.y_max());  // true 4 10
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - R `plot.default()` (`xlim`, `ylim`): https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/plot.default.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆x_limits min_x max_x
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `min_x` number: Left edge of the plotting area, in data units.
⍝ - `max_x` number: Right edge; must be greater than `min_x`.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RPlot handle: This plot, for chaining.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Unless `max_x > min_x` (NaN fails); the plot is unchanged. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `RPlot & datamunge::graphics::RPlot::x_limits(double min_x, double max_x)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆y_limits()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1036 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆RPlot∆y_limits B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Fixes the visible y range, like R's `ylim = c(min_y, max_y)`.
⍝
⍝ Without limits the y range is fitted to every layer, plus 10% padding at each end (bar
⍝ layers also include y = 0). With limits, the plotting area spans exactly
⍝ [`min_y`, `max_y`]. Data outside the range is not clipped.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ See x_limits(), which sets both ranges.
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - R `plot.default()` (`xlim`, `ylim`): https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/plot.default.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆y_limits min_y max_y
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `min_y` number: Bottom edge of the plotting area, in data units.
⍝ - `max_y` number: Top edge; must be greater than `min_y`.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RPlot handle: This plot, for chaining.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Unless `max_y > min_y` (NaN fails); the plot is unchanged. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `RPlot & datamunge::graphics::RPlot::y_limits(double min_y, double max_y)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆x_limits()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1037 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆RPlot∆xlim B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Short spelling of x_limits(), matching R's xlim plot argument. Fixes the horizontal plotting range without automatic padding. Data outside the range follows x_limits() rendering behavior; this does not filter layers.
⍝
