∇Z←H dm∆RPlot∆x_ticks B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Replaces the numeric x ticks with `labels` drawn at data positions `positions`, like R's `axis(1, at = positions, labels = labels)`.
⍝
⍝ Positions need not be sorted or inside the visible range. Two empty vectors restore the
⍝ numeric ticks.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto x = dm::seql(0.0, 6.283, 50);
⍝ auto plt = dm::Plot(x, dm::sin(x))
⍝                .x_ticks({0.0, 3.1416, 6.2832}, {"0", "pi", "2 pi"})
⍝                .y_ticks({-1.0, 0.0, 1.0}, {"min", "0", "max"});
⍝ std::print("{}\n", plt.x_tick_position_list()[1]);  // 3.1416
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - R `axis()`: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/axis.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆x_ticks positions labels
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `positions` numeric vector: x data coordinates of the ticks.
⍝ - `labels` vector of character vectors: Tick texts, one per position.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RPlot handle: This plot, for chaining.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: If the two vectors differ in length; the plot is unchanged. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `RPlot & datamunge::graphics::RPlot::x_ticks(std::vector<double> positions, std::vector<std::string> labels)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆x_tick_labels()`, `dm∆y_ticks()`
