∇Z←H dm∆RPlot∆margins B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Overrides the automatic outer margins around the plotting area, in pixels, like R's `par(mai =)` (which uses inches and orders bottom, left, top, right).
⍝
⍝ The automatic margins are left 90, right 24, top 72 (96 when a subtitle is set), and
⍝ bottom 84; they leave room for the title, axis labels, and ticks. Explicit margins are used
⍝ as given, with no extra room for a subtitle. Tight margins suit small multi-panel tiles
⍝ whose title and axis labels are empty. Rendering throws std::runtime_error if the margins
⍝ leave no plotting area.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ auto tile = dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0, 2.0), dm::c(2.0, 1.0))
⍝                 .set_title("").set_xlab("").set_ylab("")
⍝                 .margins(30, 10, 10, 20)
⍝                 .tick_font_size(9);
⍝ std::print("{} {}\n", tile.has_custom_margins(), tile.margin_left());  // true 30
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - R `par()` (`mai`, `mar`): https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/par.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆margins left right top bottom
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `left` number: Left margin in pixels.
⍝ - `right` number: Right margin in pixels.
⍝ - `top` number: Top margin in pixels.
⍝ - `bottom` number: Bottom margin in pixels.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RPlot handle: This plot, for chaining.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: If any margin is negative or non-finite; the plot is unchanged. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `RPlot & datamunge::graphics::RPlot::margins(double left, double right, double top, double bottom)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆size()`, `dm∆tick_font_size()`, `dm∆RLayout`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1045 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆RPlot∆tick_font_size B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Sets the tick-label font size in pixels (default 12), like R's `cex.axis`.
⍝
⍝ In SVG output the size is used as given. The raster outputs (PNG, PDF, show()) draw tick
⍝ labels with half-size glyphs when the size is below 12.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ See margins(), which shrinks the ticks for a small tile.
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - R `par()` (`cex.axis`): https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/par.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆tick_font_size pixels
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `pixels` number: Font size in pixels.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RPlot handle: This plot, for chaining.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: If `pixels` is not positive and finite; the plot is unchanged. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `RPlot & datamunge::graphics::RPlot::tick_font_size(double pixels)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆margins()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1046 0 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆RPlot∆pretty_ticks B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Places numeric ticks at round values (default on) instead of six evenly spaced ticks across the range, in the spirit of R's `pretty()`.
⍝
⍝ The step is 1, 2, or 5 times a power of ten, chosen as the "nice number" nearest a fifth
⍝ of the visible range (Heckbert's rule), and ticks are its multiples inside the range.
⍝ That gives about four to seven ticks, close to R's `pretty()`: a padded range of -0.365
⍝ to 7.665 gets ticks at 0, 2, 4 and 6. Custom tick labels take precedence. The background
⍝ grid follows the ticks.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ auto plt = dm::Plot(dm::c(0.0, 100.0), dm::c(0.0, 1.0)).pretty_ticks();
⍝ std::print("{}\n", plt.to_svg().contains(">40</text>"));  // true: ticks 0, 20, ..., 100
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - R `pretty()`: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/base/html/pretty.html
⍝ - P. S. Heckbert (1990). "Nice Numbers for Graph Labels". In Graphics Gems, 61-63.
⍝   Academic Press. doi:10.1016/B978-0-08-050753-8.50020-8
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆pretty_ticks ⍬
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆pretty_ticks enabled
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `enabled` boolean (0 or 1), default 1: True turns round ticks on; false restores six even ticks.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RPlot handle: This plot, for chaining.
⍝
⍝ - C++: `RPlot & datamunge::graphics::RPlot::pretty_ticks(bool enabled = true)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆x_ticks()`, `dm∆show_grid()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1047 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆RPlot∆plot B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Builds a scatter, line, or line-plus-points plot of `y` against `x`, like R's `plot(x, y, type = "p" | "l" | "b")`.
⍝
⍝ `"p"` (the default) adds one scatter series with 4-pixel markers; `"l"` adds one 2-pixel
⍝ line joining the points in input order; `"b"` adds the line and then unlabeled points on
⍝ top (R's `"b"` leaves gaps in the line around each point; this draws it through them). The
⍝ title and axis labels keep their defaults. `RPlot(x, y)` is `plot(x, y)`.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto x = dm::seql(0.0, 4.0, 5);
⍝ auto plt = dm::Plot::plot(x, x * x, "b", "x squared");
⍝ std::print("{} series\n", plt.series().size());  // 2 series
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - R `plot.default()`: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/plot.default.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆RPlot∆plot x y
⍝ Z←dm∆RPlot∆plot x y type
⍝ Z←dm∆RPlot∆plot x y type label
⍝ Z←dm∆RPlot∆plot x y type label color
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `x` numeric vector: Horizontal coordinates, one per point.
⍝ - `y` numeric vector: Vertical coordinates, paired with `x` by position.
⍝ - `type` character vector, default 'p': `"p"` (default), `"l"`, or `"b"`; case-sensitive.
⍝ - `label` character vector, default '': Legend text; empty (the default) keeps the plot out of the automatic legend. With `"b"` only the line carries it.
⍝ - `color` RGB handle, default the C++ default: Color of the points and line (default blue `{37, 99, 235}`).
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RPlot handle: A new plot holding the series.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: If the coordinates are empty or differ in length, or `type` is not one of the three codes. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `RPlot datamunge::graphics::RPlot::plot(std::vector<double> x, std::vector<double> y, std::string type = "p", std::string label = "", RGBA color = ...)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆vector<double>`, `dm∆vector<double>)`, `dm∆points()`, `dm∆lines()`
