∇Z←H dm∆RPlot∆line B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Adds a line joining the points in input order, like R's `lines(x, y, col = color, lwd = stroke_width)`; same as lines().
⍝
⍝ The points are not sorted, so unsorted x draws a zigzag. The coordinates are copied (or
⍝ moved from rvalues) into a new Line series drawn above existing layers; axis ranges grow
⍝ to include it. A nonempty `label` adds a row to the automatic legend.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ See lines().
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - R `lines()`: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/lines.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆line x y
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆line x y label
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆line x y label color
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆line x y label color stroke_width
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `x` numeric vector: Horizontal coordinates, one per vertex.
⍝ - `y` numeric vector: Vertical coordinates, paired with `x` by position.
⍝ - `label` character vector, default '': Legend text; empty (the default) keeps it out of the legend.
⍝ - `color` RGB handle, default the C++ default: Line color (default red `{220, 38, 38}`).
⍝ - `stroke_width` number, default 2.0: Line width in pixels (default 2).
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RPlot handle: This plot, for chaining.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: If the coordinates are empty or have different lengths. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `RPlot & datamunge::graphics::RPlot::line(std::vector<double> x, std::vector<double> y, std::string label = "", RGBA color = ..., double stroke_width = 2.0)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆lines()`, `dm∆plot()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1057 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
