∇Z←H dm∆RPlot∆qqline B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Adds the reference line of a normal Q-Q plot, like R's `qqline(y)`: the line through the first and third quartiles of `data` and of the standard normal.
⍝
⍝ Pass the same data given to qqnorm(). The sample quartiles are R's default "type 7"
⍝ quantiles, as in R's `qqline()` defaults (`probs = c(0.25, 0.75)`, `qtype = 7`). The
⍝ slope is the sample IQR divided by the normal IQR (about 1.349), so for normal data it
⍝ estimates the standard deviation and the intercept estimates the mean. Added through
⍝ abline().
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const std::vector<double> sample{3.0, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0, 4.0};
⍝ auto plt = dm::Plot::qqnorm(sample).qqline(sample);
⍝ const auto& line = plt.reference_lines()[0];
⍝ std::print("{:.4f} {:.4f}\n", line.value, line.slope);  // 3.0000 1.4826
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - R `qqline()`: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/qqnorm.html
⍝ - R. J. Hyndman, Y. Fan (1996). "Sample Quantiles in Statistical Packages". The American
⍝   Statistician 50(4), 361-365. doi:10.1080/00031305.1996.10473566
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆qqline data
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆qqline data color
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆qqline data color stroke_width
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `data` numeric vector: The sample; at least two values. Taken by value, so the caller's data is unchanged.
⍝ - `color` RGB handle, default the C++ default: Line color (default red `{220, 38, 38}`).
⍝ - `stroke_width` number, default 1.5: Line width in pixels (default 1.5).
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RPlot handle: This plot, for chaining.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: If `data` has fewer than two values. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - **Warning:** Only to_svg() and `.svg` saves draw reference lines. The raster outputs (`.png`, `.pdf`, and show()) currently omit them.
⍝
⍝ - C++: `RPlot & datamunge::graphics::RPlot::qqline(std::vector<double> data, RGBA color = ..., double stroke_width = 1.5)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆qqnorm()`, `dm∆abline()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1064 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆RPlot∆legend B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Adds manual legend rows, like R's `legend(legend = labels, col = colors)`.
⍝
⍝ Each label is paired with the color at the same position and drawn as a swatch row in
⍝ the legend box. Once any manual row exists, it replaces the automatic legend built from
⍝ labeled series. Calling again appends more rows. Unlike dm::legend(), colors are not
⍝ recycled here: the vectors must match in length. Empty vectors add nothing.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto up = dm::RGB{37, 99, 235};
⍝ const auto down = dm::RGB{220, 38, 38};
⍝ auto plt = dm::Plot();
⍝ plt.points(dm::c(1.0, 2.0), dm::c(1.0, 2.0), up)
⍝    .points(dm::c(1.0, 2.0), dm::c(2.0, 1.0), down)
⍝    .legend({"rising", "falling"}, {up, down});
⍝ std::print("{}\n", plt.legend_entries().size());  // 2
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - R `legend()`: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/legend.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆legend labels colors
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `labels` vector of character vectors: Row texts, in display order.
⍝ - `colors` vector of (RGB handle): Swatch colors, one per label.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RPlot handle: This plot, for chaining.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: If `labels` and `colors` differ in length. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `RPlot & datamunge::graphics::RPlot::legend(std::vector<std::string> labels, std::vector<RGBA> colors)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆legend() for the recycling`, `dm∆pipe-friendly layer.`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1065 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆RPlot∆text B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Draws `label` centered on the data point (`x`, `y`), like R's `text(x, y, labels = label)`.
⍝
⍝ The text is centered both horizontally and vertically on the point, like R's default
⍝ `adj`. The point counts toward the automatic axis ranges, but the text's extent does not,
⍝ so long labels near the edge can overflow. Text layers never appear in the legend, and
⍝ set_col() recolors them along with the data.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ auto plt = dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0, 2.0, 3.0), dm::c(1.0, 4.0, 2.0))
⍝                .text(2.0, 4.4, "peak", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 16.0);
⍝ std::print("{} {}\n", plt.series()[1].label, plt.series()[1].marker_size);  // peak 16
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - R `text()`: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/text.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆text x y label
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆text x y label color
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆text x y label color font_size
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `x` number: Horizontal position, in data units.
⍝ - `y` number: Vertical position, in data units.
⍝ - `label` character vector: The text to draw (XML-escaped in SVG output).
⍝ - `color` RGB handle, default the C++ default: Text color (default near-black `{17, 24, 39}`).
⍝ - `font_size` number, default 14.0: Font size in pixels (default 14).
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RPlot handle: This plot, for chaining.
⍝
⍝ - C++: `RPlot & datamunge::graphics::RPlot::text(double x, double y, std::string label, RGBA color = ..., double font_size = 14.0)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆pie()`, `dm∆legend()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1066 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆RPlot∆polygon B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Draws the closed polygon through the vertices (x[i], y[i]), like R's `polygon(x, y, col = color)`.
⍝
⍝ The last vertex joins the first automatically. Filled polygons (the default) are painted
⍝ with `color` (plus a 1-pixel outline in SVG; the raster fill uses the even-odd rule);
⍝ unfilled ones draw a 2-pixel outline only (R's default is unfilled). Translucent colors work well
⍝ for shaded regions such as confidence bands. Vertices count toward the axis ranges.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ auto plt = dm::Plot()
⍝                .polygon({0.0, 1.0, 0.5}, {0.0, 0.0, 1.0}, dm::RGBA{37, 99, 235, 0.4})
⍝                .polygon({0.2, 0.8, 0.8, 0.2}, {0.1, 0.1, 0.4, 0.4}, dm::RGB{0, 0, 0}, false);
⍝ std::print("{}\n", plt.series()[1].filled);  // false
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - R `polygon()`: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/polygon.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆polygon x y
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆polygon x y color
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆polygon x y color filled
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `x` numeric vector: Vertex x coordinates, in order around the polygon.
⍝ - `y` numeric vector: Vertex y coordinates, paired with `x`.
⍝ - `color` RGB handle, default the C++ default: Fill (and outline) color (default blue `{37, 99, 235}`).
⍝ - `filled` boolean (0 or 1), default 1: True (the default) fills; false draws the outline only.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RPlot handle: This plot, for chaining.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: If the coordinates are empty or have different lengths. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `RPlot & datamunge::graphics::RPlot::polygon(std::vector<double> x, std::vector<double> y, RGBA color = ..., bool filled = true)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆pie()`, `dm∆segments()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1067 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆RPlot∆image B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Draws a grid of coloured rectangles, one per value of `z`, like R's `image(x, y, z)`.
⍝
⍝ Cell (i, j) is centred on (x[i], y[j]) and reaches halfway to its neighbours (a half step
⍝ beyond the outermost centres). Its colour is the palette entry for z[i][j] on a linear
⍝ scale from `z_min` to `z_max`; NaN cells are left empty. Each cell is added as a filled
⍝ polygon, so the grid should have at most tens of thousands of cells.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ auto plt = dm::graphics::RPlot::create().image({0.0, 1.0}, {0.0, 1.0, 2.0}, {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}});
⍝ std::print("{}\n", plt.series().size());  // 6
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - R `image()`: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/image.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆image x y z
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆image x y z palette
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆image x y z palette z_min
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆image x y z palette z_min z_max
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `x` numeric vector: Cell centres along x, increasing (at least 2).
⍝ - `y` numeric vector: Cell centres along y, increasing (at least 2).
⍝ - `z` numeric matrix (one row per element): Values, z[i][j] at (x[i], y[j]); z.size() == x.size() and each row has y.size() entries.
⍝ - `palette` vector of (RGB handle), default the C++ default: Colours from low to high (default terrain_colors(12)).
⍝ - `z_min` number, default the C++ default: Value mapped to the first colour; NaN (the default) uses the smallest z.
⍝ - `z_max` number, default the C++ default: Value mapped to the last colour; NaN (the default) uses the largest z.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RPlot handle: This plot, for chaining.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: If the sizes do not match, x or y is not increasing, or the palette is empty. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `RPlot & datamunge::graphics::RPlot::image(const std::vector<double> & x, const std::vector<double> & y, const std::vector<std::vector<double>> & z, std::vector<RGBA> palette = {}, double z_min = ..., double z_max = ...)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆terrain_colors()`, `dm∆polygon()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1068 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆RPlot∆segments B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Draws one straight segment from (x0[i], y0[i]) to (x1[i], y1[i]) for each i, like R's `segments(x0, y0, x1, y1)`.
⍝
⍝ All segments share `color` and `stroke_width` and form one Segment layer; their end
⍝ points count toward the axis ranges. Useful for error bars, residual sticks, and
⍝ dendrograms.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto x = dm::c(1.0, 2.0, 3.0);
⍝ const auto y = dm::c(2.0, 3.5, 3.0);
⍝ auto plt = dm::Plot(x, y).segments(x, y - 0.5, x, y + 0.5);  // +/- 0.5 error bars
⍝ std::print("{}\n", plt.series()[1].x.size());  // 6 (start, end, start, end, ...)
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - R `segments()`: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/segments.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆segments x0 y0 x1 y1
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆segments x0 y0 x1 y1 color
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RPlot∆segments x0 y0 x1 y1 color stroke_width
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `x0` numeric vector: Start x coordinates.
⍝ - `y0` numeric vector: Start y coordinates.
⍝ - `x1` numeric vector: End x coordinates.
⍝ - `y1` numeric vector: End y coordinates.
⍝ - `color` RGB handle, default the C++ default: Segment color (default opaque black).
⍝ - `stroke_width` number, default 1.0: Segment width in pixels (default 1).
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RPlot handle: This plot, for chaining.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: If the four vectors differ in length or are empty. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `RPlot & datamunge::graphics::RPlot::segments(std::vector<double> x0, std::vector<double> y0, std::vector<double> x1, std::vector<double> y1, RGBA color = ..., double stroke_width = 1.0)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆abline()`, `dm∆polygon()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1069 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆RLayout∆new B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Tiles independently built plots into one multi-panel figure, like R's `par(mfrow = c(rows, cols))` followed by one plot per panel.
⍝
⍝ Create it with create(rows, cols), then add() up to rows * cols panels; they fill the grid
⍝ row by row. Each panel is copied (and its SVG rendered) at add() time, so later changes to
⍝ the original plot do not show and panels need not outlive the layout. Each cell is
⍝ `width / cols` by `(height - band) / rows` pixels, where band is title_band_px when a
⍝ figure title is set and 0 otherwise; every panel is scaled from its own canvas size into its
⍝ cell. The figure background is white. Unused cells stay empty.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto x = dm::seql(0.0, 6.0, 30);
⍝ auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2)
⍝                .size(1200, 500)
⍝                .title("Two views")
⍝                .add(dm::Plot(x, dm::sin(x)).set_title("sin"))
⍝                .add(dm::Plot(x, dm::cos(x)).set_title("cos"));
⍝ std::print("{} panels in {}x{}\n", fig.panel_count(), fig.rows(), fig.cols());
⍝ // 2 panels in 1x2
⍝ fig.save("two_views.svg");
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - R `par()` (`mfrow`): https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/par.html
⍝
⍝ **Members:** `dm∆RLayout∆create` (static), `dm∆RLayout∆add`, `dm∆RLayout∆size`, `dm∆RLayout∆title`, `dm∆RLayout∆rows`, `dm∆RLayout∆cols`, `dm∆RLayout∆width`, `dm∆RLayout∆height`, `dm∆RLayout∆panel_count`, `dm∆RLayout∆title_text`, `dm∆RLayout∆panel`, `dm∆RLayout∆to_svg`, `dm∆RLayout∆save`, `dm∆RLayout∆save_svg`, `dm∆RLayout∆to_png`, `dm∆RLayout∆to_pdf`, `dm∆RLayout∆save_png`, `dm∆RLayout∆save_pdf`, `dm∆RLayout∆fit_panels`, `dm∆RLayout∆show`, `dm∆RLayout∆view`.
⍝
⍝ Handles are `('RLayout' id)`; release them with `dm∆Delete obj`.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ obj←dm∆RLayout∆new
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RLayout handle
⍝
⍝ - C++: `datamunge::graphics::RLayout::RLayout()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(¯2 2 1094) dm⍙native ⍬
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆RLayout∆title_band_px;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Height in pixels of the heading band reserved above the panels when a title is set.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:** `Z←dm∆RLayout∆title_band_px`
⍝
⍝ **Result:** integer
⍝
⍝ - C++: `datamunge::graphics::RLayout::title_band_px`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1092 2) dm⍙native ⍬
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆RLayout∆min_panel_px;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Smallest canvas side fit_panels() gives a panel; smaller cells get a larger canvas with the cell's aspect ratio, scaled down when drawn, so the margins still fit.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:** `Z←dm∆RLayout∆min_panel_px`
⍝
⍝ **Result:** integer
⍝
⍝ - C++: `datamunge::graphics::RLayout::min_panel_px`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1093 2) dm⍙native ⍬
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆RLayout∆create B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Creates an empty layout with `rows` by `cols` cells, like R's `par(mfrow = c(rows, cols))`.
⍝
⍝ The figure starts at 960 by 640 pixels with no title.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ See RLayout.
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - R `par()` (`mfrow`): https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/par.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆RLayout∆create rows cols
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `rows` integer: Number of panel rows; must be positive.
⍝ - `cols` integer: Number of panel columns; must be positive.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RLayout handle: The empty layout.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: If `rows` or `cols` is 0. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `RLayout datamunge::graphics::RLayout::create(std::size_t rows, std::size_t cols)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆add()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1071 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆RLayout∆add B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Copies `panel` into the next free cell, filling rows left to right, top to bottom.
⍝
⍝ The panel's SVG is rendered immediately and stored with the copy, so rendering errors
⍝ surface here and later edits to the original plot have no effect on the layout.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 1).add(dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0, 2.0), dm::c(2.0, 1.0)));
⍝ try {
⍝   fig.add(dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0), dm::c(1.0)));
⍝ } catch (const std::invalid_argument& e) {
⍝   std::print("{}\n", e.what());  // RLayout::add: layout is already full
⍝ }
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RLayout∆add panel
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `panel` Plot handle: The plot to add; any RPlot (or other Plot) converts.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RLayout handle: This layout, for chaining.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: If all rows * cols cells are already filled. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: native: …`: If `panel` has no data series or its canvas is smaller than its margins. (C++ `std::runtime_error`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `RLayout & datamunge::graphics::RLayout::add(const Plot & panel)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆panel()`, `dm∆panel_count()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1072 0 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆RLayout∆size B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Sets the size of the whole figure in pixels (default 960 by 640).
⍝
⍝ Panels keep their own canvas sizes and are scaled into their cells, so text in a panel
⍝ shrinks or grows with it; give panels a size close to their cell for readable labels.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ See RLayout.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RLayout∆size width height
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `width` integer: Figure width in pixels.
⍝ - `height` integer: Figure height in pixels, including the title band.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RLayout handle: This layout, for chaining.
⍝
⍝ - C++: `RLayout & datamunge::graphics::RLayout::size(std::size_t width, std::size_t height)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆title()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1073 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

