∇Z←dm∆AMSGrad∆new B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ AMSGrad on the DifferentiableFunction callback interface. Bias-corrected AMSGrad variant: maximises uncorrected second moments, then divides that maximum by 1-beta2^t. The corrected denominator need not be monotone, so the original uncorrected theorem is not automatically inherited. Tests the gradient used after updating; O(p) reset state. The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed; coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws. Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone is not a uniform convergence certificate.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const dm::optim::AMSGrad optimizer;
⍝ std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 10000
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - S. Reddi, S. Kale and S. Kumar (2018), On the Convergence of Adam and Beyond, https://openreview.net/forum?id=ryQu7f-RZ
⍝
⍝ **Members:** `dm∆AMSGrad∆optimize`, `dm∆AMSGrad∆options`.
⍝
⍝ Handles are `('AMSGrad' id)`; release them with `dm∆Delete obj`.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ obj←dm∆AMSGrad∆new ⍬
⍝ obj←dm∆AMSGrad∆new options
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `options` AMSGradOptions handle, default the C++ default: Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by AMSGradOptions.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** AMSGrad handle
⍝
⍝ - C++: `datamunge::optim::AMSGrad::AMSGrad(AMSGradOptions options = {})`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(¯2 1 1272) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
