∇Z←dm∆BayesianSurrogate∆new B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Surrogate/acquisition extension point for BayesianOptimization.
⍝
⍝ A surrogate is refitted to all observations before every proposal. acquisition() scores a
⍝ candidate point; the optimizer evaluates the objective where the score is largest.
⍝ RBFGaussianProcessSurrogate is the built-in implementation.
⍝
⍝ Abstract in C++: implement it in APL with `dm∆BayesianSurrogate∆subclass`.
⍝
⍝ **Members:** `dm∆BayesianSurrogate∆fit`, `dm∆BayesianSurrogate∆acquisition`.
⍝
⍝ Handles are `('BayesianSurrogate' id)`; release them with `dm∆Delete obj`.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ obj←dm∆BayesianSurrogate∆new
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Result:** BayesianSurrogate handle
⍝
⍝ - C++: `datamunge::optim::BayesianSurrogate::BayesianSurrogate()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(¯2 2 1303) dm⍙native ⍬
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←P dm∆BayesianSurrogate∆subclass B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ An APL implementation of `dm∆BayesianSurrogate`: C++ calls the APL functions Impl∆method for its virtual methods.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ obj←'Impl' dm∆BayesianSurrogate∆subclass
⍝ ```
⍝ `Impl` is a name prefix: for each overridable method below, a defined function `Impl∆method` overrides it,
⍝ called as `Z←obj Impl∆method args` (`Z←Impl∆method obj` without arguments, the same conventions as the
⍝ generated functions). Methods without an APL function run the C++ implementation.
⍝
⍝ **Overridable:**
⍝ - `fit` (abstract: required) — C++ `void datamunge::optim::BayesianSurrogate::fit(const std::vector<std::vector<double>> & points, const std::vector<double> & values)`
⍝ - `acquisition` (abstract: required) — C++ `double datamunge::optim::BayesianSurrogate::acquisition(const std::vector<double> & point, double incumbent)`
⍝
⍝ The object lives until `dm∆Delete obj`, or while C++ owns it.
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(¯2 2 1303 P) dm⍙native ⍬
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
