∇Z←dm∆CoordinateDescent∆new B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ CoordinateDescent on the DifferentiableFunction callback interface. Each epoch visits coordinates in index order. Every partial update requests a full gradient and an Armijo-backtracked value sequence; this is not exact one-dimensional minimisation. An exhausted search restores that coordinate. Stops on the full end-of-epoch gradient norm. The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed; coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws. Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone is not a uniform convergence certificate.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const dm::optim::CoordinateDescent optimizer;
⍝ std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 1000
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - S. J. Wright (2015), Coordinate descent algorithms, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10107-015-0892-3. This implementation uses the stated full-gradient coordinate/block steps.
⍝
⍝ **Members:** `dm∆CoordinateDescent∆optimize`, `dm∆CoordinateDescent∆options`.
⍝
⍝ Handles are `('CoordinateDescent' id)`; release them with `dm∆Delete obj`.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ obj←dm∆CoordinateDescent∆new ⍬
⍝ obj←dm∆CoordinateDescent∆new options
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
