∇Z←dm∆FISTA∆new B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ FISTA on the ProximalFunction callback interface. Classical t recurrence (1+sqrt(1+4*t^2))/2 and extrapolated gradient points, followed by the supplied proximal map. Stops on successive accepted-coordinate displacement. No adaptive restart, backtracking or monotonicity safeguard. Supply the correct proximal dimensions and a rate at most 1/L for the standard convex bound. The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed; coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws. Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone is not a uniform convergence certificate.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const dm::optim::FISTA optimizer;
⍝ std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 10000
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - A. Beck and M. Teboulle (2009), A Fast Iterative Shrinkage-Thresholding Algorithm for Linear Inverse Problems, https://doi.org/10.1137/080716542
⍝
⍝ **Members:** `dm∆FISTA∆optimize`, `dm∆FISTA∆options`.
⍝
⍝ Handles are `('FISTA' id)`; release them with `dm∆Delete obj`.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ obj←dm∆FISTA∆new ⍬
⍝ obj←dm∆FISTA∆new options
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `options` FISTAOptions handle, default the C++ default: Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by FISTAOptions.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** FISTA handle
⍝
⍝ - C++: `datamunge::optim::FISTA::FISTA(FISTAOptions options = {})`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(¯2 1 1497) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆FISTA∆optimize B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Run FISTA from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Classical t recurrence (1+sqrt(1+4*t^2))/2 and extrapolated gradient points, followed by the supplied proximal map. Stops on successive accepted-coordinate displacement. No adaptive restart, backtracking or monotonicity safeguard. Supply the correct proximal dimensions and a rate at most 1/L for the standard convex bound.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ dm::optim::L1LeastSquaresObjective f({{1}},{3},1);
⍝ dm::optim::FISTAOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
⍝ std::vector<double> x{0};
⍝ const auto value=dm::optim::FISTA(controls).optimize(f,x);
⍝ std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆FISTA∆optimize function coordinates
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `function` ProximalFunction handle: Borrowed ProximalFunction with matching finite output shapes.
⍝ - `coordinates` numeric vector (updated: returned in Z): Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native returned point.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** the result, then the updated arguments (one value alone, several as a vector): Objective at the native returned coordinates; not a stop-status code.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: FISTA: step_size must be positive. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: native: …`: Exceptions from supplied callbacks propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite inputs. (C++ `std::exception`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `double datamunge::optim::FISTA::optimize(ProximalFunction & function, std::vector<double> & coordinates) const`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆trace_optimize()`, `dm∆FISTAOptions`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1495 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

