∇Z←dm∆GradientDescent∆new B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ GradientDescent on the DifferentiableFunction callback interface. Velocity is momentum*velocity-step_size*gradient. Updates before testing the gradient just used or absolute objective change. Equal objective values can stop an oscillation away from stationarity. O(p) reset velocity state; no line search. The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed; coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws. Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone is not a uniform convergence certificate.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const dm::optim::GradientDescent optimizer;
⍝ std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 10000
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - J. Nocedal and S. J. Wright (2006), Numerical Optimization, second edition, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-40065-5. The implemented variant and stopping rules are specified above.
⍝
⍝ **Members:** `dm∆GradientDescent∆optimize`, `dm∆GradientDescent∆options`.
⍝
⍝ Handles are `('GradientDescent' id)`; release them with `dm∆Delete obj`.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ obj←dm∆GradientDescent∆new ⍬
⍝ obj←dm∆GradientDescent∆new options
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `options` GradientDescentOptions handle, default the C++ default: Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by GradientDescentOptions.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** GradientDescent handle
⍝
⍝ - C++: `datamunge::optim::GradientDescent::GradientDescent(GradientDescentOptions options = {})`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(¯2 1 1539) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆GradientDescent∆optimize B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Run GradientDescent from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Velocity is momentum*velocity-step_size*gradient. Updates before testing the gradient just used or absolute objective change. Equal objective values can stop an oscillation away from stationarity. O(p) reset velocity state; no line search.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ dm::optim::QuadraticObjective f({2,8},{1,-2});
⍝ dm::optim::GradientDescentOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
⍝ std::vector<double> x{0,0};
⍝ const auto value=dm::optim::GradientDescent(controls).optimize(f,x);
⍝ std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆GradientDescent∆optimize function coordinates
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `function` DifferentiableFunction handle: Borrowed DifferentiableFunction with matching finite output shapes.
⍝ - `coordinates` numeric vector (updated: returned in Z): Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native returned point.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** the result, then the updated arguments (one value alone, several as a vector): Objective at the native returned coordinates; not a stop-status code.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: native: …`: Exceptions from supplied callbacks propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite inputs. (C++ `std::exception`)
⍝
