∇Z←dm∆LevenbergMarquardt∆new B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ LevenbergMarquardt on the ResidualFunction callback interface. Forms J transpose J plus scalar damping*I and solves by unpivoted elimination. Accepts only strict reductions of half residual SSE, multiplying damping by .3 on acceptance and 10 on rejection. Tests proposed step norm even after rejection. A small rejected step is not a gradient certificate; no parameter constraints. O(np^2+p^3) work. The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed; coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws. Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone is not a uniform convergence certificate.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const dm::optim::LevenbergMarquardt optimizer;
⍝ std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 200
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - K. Levenberg (1944), A Method for the Solution of Certain Non-Linear Problems in Least Squares, https://doi.org/10.1090/qam/10666; D. W. Marquardt (1963), An Algorithm for Least-Squares Estimation of Nonlinear Parameters, https://doi.org/10.1137/0111030
⍝
⍝ **Members:** `dm∆LevenbergMarquardt∆optimize`, `dm∆LevenbergMarquardt∆options`.
⍝
⍝ Handles are `('LevenbergMarquardt' id)`; release them with `dm∆Delete obj`.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ obj←dm∆LevenbergMarquardt∆new ⍬
⍝ obj←dm∆LevenbergMarquardt∆new options
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `options` LevenbergMarquardtOptions handle, default the C++ default: Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by LevenbergMarquardtOptions.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** LevenbergMarquardt handle
⍝
⍝ - C++: `datamunge::optim::LevenbergMarquardt::LevenbergMarquardt(LevenbergMarquardtOptions options = {})`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(¯2 1 1609) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆LevenbergMarquardt∆optimize B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Run LevenbergMarquardt from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Forms J transpose J plus scalar damping*I and solves by unpivoted elimination. Accepts only strict reductions of half residual SSE, multiplying damping by .3 on acceptance and 10 on rejection. Tests proposed step norm even after rejection. A small rejected step is not a gradient certificate; no parameter constraints. O(np^2+p^3) work.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto data=dm::optim::optimization_curve_data();dm::optim::CurveResidual f(data);
⍝ dm::optim::LevenbergMarquardtOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
⍝ std::vector<double> x{2,.5};
⍝ const auto value=dm::optim::LevenbergMarquardt(controls).optimize(f,x);
⍝ std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆LevenbergMarquardt∆optimize function coordinates
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `function` ResidualFunction handle: Borrowed ResidualFunction with matching finite output shapes.
⍝ - `coordinates` numeric vector (updated: returned in Z): Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native returned point.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** the result, then the updated arguments (one value alone, several as a vector): Objective at the native returned coordinates (half residual SSE); not a stop-status code.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: LevenbergMarquardt: initial_damping must be positive. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: native: …`: Exceptions from supplied callbacks propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite inputs. (C++ `std::exception`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `double datamunge::optim::LevenbergMarquardt::optimize(ResidualFunction & function, std::vector<double> & coordinates) const`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆trace_optimize()`, `dm∆LevenbergMarquardtOptions`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1607 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
