∇Z←dm∆NelderMead∆new B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ NelderMead on the ArbitraryFunction callback interface. Local simplex reflection, expansion, contraction and shrink search. Starts with per-coordinate displacements initial_simplex_scale and stops when simplex diameter and value spread are small. Those conditions do not prove stationarity or a global minimum. O(p^2) simplex storage. The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed; coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws. Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone is not a uniform convergence certificate.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const dm::optim::NelderMead optimizer;
⍝ std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 2000
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - J. A. Nelder and R. Mead (1965), A Simplex Method for Function Minimization, https://doi.org/10.1093/comjnl/7.4.308
⍝
⍝ **Members:** `dm∆NelderMead∆optimize`, `dm∆NelderMead∆options`.
⍝
⍝ Handles are `('NelderMead' id)`; release them with `dm∆Delete obj`.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ obj←dm∆NelderMead∆new ⍬
⍝ obj←dm∆NelderMead∆new options
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `options` NelderMeadOptions handle, default the C++ default: Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by NelderMeadOptions.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** NelderMead handle
⍝
⍝ - C++: `datamunge::optim::NelderMead::NelderMead(NelderMeadOptions options = {})`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(¯2 1 1627) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆NelderMead∆optimize B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Run NelderMead from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Local simplex reflection, expansion, contraction and shrink search. Starts with per-coordinate displacements initial_simplex_scale and stops when simplex diameter and value spread are small. Those conditions do not prove stationarity or a global minimum. O(p^2) simplex storage.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ dm::optim::QuadraticObjective f({2,8},{1,-2});
⍝ dm::optim::NelderMeadOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
⍝ std::vector<double> x{0,0};
⍝ const auto value=dm::optim::NelderMead(controls).optimize(f,x);
⍝ std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆NelderMead∆optimize function coordinates
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `function` ArbitraryFunction handle: Borrowed ArbitraryFunction with matching finite output shapes.
⍝ - `coordinates` numeric vector (updated: returned in Z): Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native returned point.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** the result, then the updated arguments (one value alone, several as a vector): Objective at the native returned coordinates; not a stop-status code.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: NelderMead: initial_simplex_scale must be positive; NelderMead: reflection must be positive; NelderMead: expansion must be greater than 1; NelderMead: contraction must be in (0, 1); NelderMead: shrink must be in (0, 1); NelderMead: tolerance must be non-negative. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: native: …`: Exceptions from supplied callbacks propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite inputs. (C++ `std::exception`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `double datamunge::optim::NelderMead::optimize(ArbitraryFunction & function, std::vector<double> & coordinates) const`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆trace_optimize()`, `dm∆NelderMeadOptions`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1625 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

