∇Z←dm∆ProximalGradient∆new B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ ProximalGradient on the ProximalFunction callback interface. ISTA update prox(x-step_size*gradient(x),step_size), with gradient of the smooth part only. Stops on coordinate displacement norm. There is no Lipschitz estimation or backtracking; caller chooses a stable rate. The returned objective includes both smooth and nonsmooth parts. The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed; coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws. Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone is not a uniform convergence certificate.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const dm::optim::ProximalGradient optimizer;
⍝ std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 10000
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - A. Beck and M. Teboulle (2009), A Fast Iterative Shrinkage-Thresholding Algorithm for Linear Inverse Problems, https://doi.org/10.1137/080716542
⍝
⍝ **Members:** `dm∆ProximalGradient∆optimize`, `dm∆ProximalGradient∆options`.
⍝
⍝ Handles are `('ProximalGradient' id)`; release them with `dm∆Delete obj`.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ obj←dm∆ProximalGradient∆new ⍬
⍝ obj←dm∆ProximalGradient∆new options
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `options` ProximalGradientOptions handle, default the C++ default: Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by ProximalGradientOptions.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** ProximalGradient handle
⍝
⍝ - C++: `datamunge::optim::ProximalGradient::ProximalGradient(ProximalGradientOptions options = {})`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(¯2 1 1719) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆ProximalGradient∆optimize B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Run ProximalGradient from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. ISTA update prox(x-step_size*gradient(x),step_size), with gradient of the smooth part only. Stops on coordinate displacement norm. There is no Lipschitz estimation or backtracking; caller chooses a stable rate. The returned objective includes both smooth and nonsmooth parts.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ dm::optim::L1LeastSquaresObjective f({{1}},{3},1);
⍝ dm::optim::ProximalGradientOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
⍝ std::vector<double> x{0};
⍝ const auto value=dm::optim::ProximalGradient(controls).optimize(f,x);
⍝ std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆ProximalGradient∆optimize function coordinates
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `function` ProximalFunction handle: Borrowed ProximalFunction with matching finite output shapes.
⍝ - `coordinates` numeric vector (updated: returned in Z): Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native returned point.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** the result, then the updated arguments (one value alone, several as a vector): Objective at the native returned coordinates; not a stop-status code.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: ProximalGradient: step_size must be positive; ProximalGradient: proximal returned wrong dimension. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: native: …`: Exceptions from supplied callbacks propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite inputs. (C++ `std::exception`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `double datamunge::optim::ProximalGradient::optimize(ProximalFunction & function, std::vector<double> & coordinates) const`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆trace_optimize()`, `dm∆ProximalGradientOptions`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1717 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
