∇Z←dm∆RMSProp∆new B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ RMSProp on the DifferentiableFunction callback interface. Uses an exponential squared-gradient average with decay_rate, dividing by sqrt(average)+epsilon. Tests the pre-update gradient norm after updating. Fixed rates can oscillate. O(p) local state, reset on every call. The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed; coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws. Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone is not a uniform convergence certificate.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const dm::optim::RMSProp optimizer;
⍝ std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 10000
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - G. Hinton (2012), lecture 6e, https://www.cs.utoronto.ca/~hinton/coursera_lectures.html
⍝
⍝ **Members:** `dm∆RMSProp∆optimize`, `dm∆RMSProp∆options`.
⍝
⍝ Handles are `('RMSProp' id)`; release them with `dm∆Delete obj`.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ obj←dm∆RMSProp∆new ⍬
⍝ obj←dm∆RMSProp∆new options
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `options` RMSPropOptions handle, default the C++ default: Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by RMSPropOptions.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RMSProp handle
⍝
⍝ - C++: `datamunge::optim::RMSProp::RMSProp(RMSPropOptions options = {})`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(¯2 1 1753) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆RMSProp∆optimize B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Run RMSProp from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Uses an exponential squared-gradient average with decay_rate, dividing by sqrt(average)+epsilon. Tests the pre-update gradient norm after updating. Fixed rates can oscillate. O(p) local state, reset on every call.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ dm::optim::QuadraticObjective f({2,8},{1,-2});
⍝ dm::optim::RMSPropOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
⍝ std::vector<double> x{0,0};
⍝ const auto value=dm::optim::RMSProp(controls).optimize(f,x);
⍝ std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆RMSProp∆optimize function coordinates
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `function` DifferentiableFunction handle: Borrowed DifferentiableFunction with matching finite output shapes.
⍝ - `coordinates` numeric vector (updated: returned in Z): Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native returned point.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** the result, then the updated arguments (one value alone, several as a vector): Objective at the native returned coordinates; not a stop-status code.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: RMSProp: step_size must be positive; RMSProp: decay_rate must be in [0, 1); RMSProp: epsilon must be positive; RMSProp: tolerance must be non-negative. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: native: …`: Exceptions from supplied callbacks propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite inputs. (C++ `std::exception`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `double datamunge::optim::RMSProp::optimize(DifferentiableFunction & function, std::vector<double> & coordinates) const`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆trace_optimize()`, `dm∆RMSPropOptions`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1751 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

