∇Z←dm∆SGD∆new B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ SGD on the DifferentiableSeparableFunction callback interface. Uses the mean gradient of each mini-batch, reshuffling component indices each epoch when enabled. Full objective remains the sum of components. Stops on absolute full-objective change per epoch. Nonempty components and positive batch_size are required; seeds reproduce the same build, not a cross-library shuffle transcript. The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed; coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws. Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone is not a uniform convergence certificate.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const dm::optim::SGD optimizer;
⍝ std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_epochs); // 100
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - H. Robbins and S. Monro (1951), A Stochastic Approximation Method, https://doi.org/10.1214/aoms/1177729586. The fixed-rate reshuffling variant here differs from their decreasing-step stochastic approximation.
⍝
⍝ **Members:** `dm∆SGD∆optimize`, `dm∆SGD∆options`.
⍝
⍝ Handles are `('SGD' id)`; release them with `dm∆Delete obj`.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ obj←dm∆SGD∆new ⍬
⍝ obj←dm∆SGD∆new options
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `options` SGDOptions handle, default the C++ default: Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by SGDOptions.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** SGD handle
⍝
⍝ - C++: `datamunge::optim::SGD::SGD(SGDOptions options = {})`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(¯2 1 1810) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆SGD∆optimize B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Run SGD from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Uses the mean gradient of each mini-batch, reshuffling component indices each epoch when enabled. Full objective remains the sum of components. Stops on absolute full-objective change per epoch. Nonempty components and positive batch_size are required; seeds reproduce the same build, not a cross-library shuffle transcript.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ dm::optim::LeastSquaresObjective f({{1},{1}},{1,3});
⍝ dm::optim::SGDOptions controls;controls.max_epochs=5;
⍝ std::vector<double> x{0};
⍝ const auto value=dm::optim::SGD(controls).optimize(f,x);
⍝ std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆SGD∆optimize function coordinates
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `function` DifferentiableSeparableFunction handle: Borrowed DifferentiableSeparableFunction with matching finite output shapes.
⍝ - `coordinates` numeric vector (updated: returned in Z): Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native returned point.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** the result, then the updated arguments (one value alone, several as a vector): Objective at the native returned coordinates; not a stop-status code.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: SGD: batch_size must be positive; SGD::optimize: function has zero terms. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: native: …`: Exceptions from supplied callbacks propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite inputs. (C++ `std::exception`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `double datamunge::optim::SGD::optimize(DifferentiableSeparableFunction & function, std::vector<double> & coordinates) const`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆trace_optimize()`, `dm∆SGDOptions`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1808 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

