∇Z←dm∆SAGA∆new B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ SAGA on the DifferentiableSeparableFunction callback interface. Uses current_i-table_i+mean(table), then refreshes one stored component gradient and its running mean. The estimator targets mean component loss while evaluate() returns their sum. Stops on epoch-boundary objective change or the last estimator norm; the latter is not the full gradient norm. O(n*p) gradient table. The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed; coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws. Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone is not a uniform convergence certificate.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const dm::optim::SAGA optimizer;
⍝ std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 10000
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - A. Defazio, F. Bach and S. Lacoste-Julien (2014), SAGA: A Fast Incremental Gradient Method With Support for Non-Strongly Convex Composite Objectives, https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.0202
⍝
⍝ **Members:** `dm∆SAGA∆optimize`, `dm∆SAGA∆options`.
⍝
⍝ Handles are `('SAGA' id)`; release them with `dm∆Delete obj`.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ obj←dm∆SAGA∆new ⍬
⍝ obj←dm∆SAGA∆new options
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `options` SAGAOptions handle, default the C++ default: Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by SAGAOptions.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** SAGA handle
⍝
⍝ - C++: `datamunge::optim::SAGA::SAGA(SAGAOptions options = {})`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(¯2 1 1836) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←H dm∆SAGA∆optimize B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Run SAGA from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Uses current_i-table_i+mean(table), then refreshes one stored component gradient and its running mean. The estimator targets mean component loss while evaluate() returns their sum. Stops on epoch-boundary objective change or the last estimator norm; the latter is not the full gradient norm. O(n*p) gradient table.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ dm::optim::LeastSquaresObjective f({{1},{1}},{1,3});
⍝ dm::optim::SAGAOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
⍝ std::vector<double> x{0};
⍝ const auto value=dm::optim::SAGA(controls).optimize(f,x);
⍝ std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆SAGA∆optimize function coordinates
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `function` DifferentiableSeparableFunction handle: Borrowed DifferentiableSeparableFunction with matching finite output shapes.
⍝ - `coordinates` numeric vector (updated: returned in Z): Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native returned point.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** the result, then the updated arguments (one value alone, several as a vector): Objective at the native returned coordinates; not a stop-status code.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: SAGA: step_size must be positive; SAGA: tolerance must be non-negative; SAGA: function has zero terms. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: native: …`: Exceptions from supplied callbacks propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite inputs. (C++ `std::exception`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `double datamunge::optim::SAGA::optimize(DifferentiableSeparableFunction & function, std::vector<double> & coordinates) const`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆trace_optimize()`, `dm∆SAGAOptions`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(1834 1 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

