∇Z←H dm∆BSplineRefinement∆apply B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Maps coarse coefficients to the refined basis.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ auto r = dm::fda::dyadic_refine(dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(1, 2));
⍝ std::print("{}\n", r.apply({0.0, 2.0})); // [0, 1, 2]
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←obj dm∆BSplineRefinement∆apply coefficients
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `coefficients` numeric vector: One coefficient per coarse basis function.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** numeric vector: One coefficient per refined basis function, representing the same spline.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: If `coefficients` does not match the coarse basis size. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `std::vector<double> datamunge::fda::BSplineRefinement::apply(const std::vector<double> & coefficients) const`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(2973 0 H) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆TensorBSpline∆new B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ A scalar tensor-product B-spline f(x) = sum_j c_j B_j(x) with owned coefficients.
⍝
⍝ With one basis this is an ordinary spline curve; with d bases a surface or volume. The
⍝ coefficient net is row-major over the per-axis basis sizes (last axis fastest), the same
⍝ column order as fda::BSpline::evaluate(). Evaluation clamps each coordinate into the
⍝ basis domain, so points outside the domain take the boundary value. Const methods are
⍝ safe to call concurrently while the object is not mutated.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(1, 2);     // linear on [0, 1]
⍝ dm::fda::TensorBSpline f({b, b}, {0, 1, 1, 2});         // f(x, y) = x + y
⍝ std::print("{:.2f}\n", f.evaluate({0.25, 0.5}));        // 0.75
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - C. de Boor (1972). On calculating with B-splines. Journal of Approximation Theory
⍝   6(1), 50-62. https://doi.org/10.1016/0021-9045(72)90080-9
⍝
⍝ **Members:** `dm∆TensorBSpline∆dimensions`, `dm∆TensorBSpline∆bases`, `dm∆TensorBSpline∆shape`, `dm∆TensorBSpline∆coefficient_count`, `dm∆TensorBSpline∆coefficients`, `dm∆TensorBSpline∆domain_min`, `dm∆TensorBSpline∆domain_max`, `dm∆TensorBSpline∆coefficient`, `dm∆TensorBSpline∆set_coefficient`, `dm∆TensorBSpline∆evaluate`, `dm∆TensorBSpline∆evaluate_points`, `dm∆TensorBSpline∆evaluate_grid`, `dm∆TensorBSpline∆dyadic_refine`.
⍝
⍝ Handles are `('TensorBSpline' id)`; release them with `dm∆Delete obj`.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ obj←dm∆TensorBSpline∆new basis coefficients
⍝ obj←dm∆TensorBSpline∆new bases coefficients
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ One-dimensional spline curve on `basis`.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** TensorBSpline handle
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: If the coefficient count differs from basis.basis_size(). (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `datamunge::fda::TensorBSpline::TensorBSpline(BSplineBasis basis, std::vector<double> coefficients)`
⍝ - C++: `datamunge::fda::TensorBSpline::TensorBSpline(std::vector<BSplineBasis> bases, std::vector<double> coefficients)`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(¯2 1 2990) dm⍙native B
