∇Z←dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆new B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Truncated hierarchical B-spline (THB-spline) of Giannelli, Juttler and Speleers.
⍝
⍝ Level 0 is the caller's TensorBSpline; level l + 1 is the dyadic refinement of level l.
⍝ A dyadic cell tree defines the nested domains Omega^0 (whole domain) contains Omega^1
⍝ contains ..., where Omega^l is the union of cells of depth >= l. A level-l basis
⍝ function is active when its support lies in Omega^l but not in Omega^(l+1). The THB
⍝ basis consists of the active functions, each truncated: its representation on finer
⍝ levels drops the finer functions whose support lies in the finer refined domain. The
⍝ resulting basis is a partition of unity, preserves the continuity of the underlying
⍝ splines, and spans the hierarchical spline space.
⍝
⍝ Internally each level stores a full coefficient net whose entries outside the next
⍝ level's refined domain always equal the refinement of the coarser level; coefficient
⍝ edits are pushed down through all finer levels with truncation, so evaluation needs
⍝ only the finest level covering a point. Refinement never changes the represented
⍝ function. Bases must be clamped. Cells are integer ids (root 0).
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(2, 4);
⍝ dm::fda::TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline thb(dm::fda::TensorBSpline({b, b}, std::vector<double>(16, 1.0)));
⍝ thb.refine_region({0.0, 0.0}, {0.5, 0.5}, 1);
⍝ std::print("{} {:.3f}\n", thb.level_count(), thb.evaluate({0.2, 0.2})); // 2 1.000
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - C. Giannelli, B. Juttler and H. Speleers (2012). THB-splines: The truncated basis for
⍝   hierarchical splines. Computer Aided Geometric Design 29(7), 485-498.
⍝   https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cagd.2012.03.025
⍝ - D. R. Forsey and R. H. Bartels (1988). Hierarchical B-spline refinement.
⍝   https://doi.org/10.1145/378456.378512
⍝
⍝ **Members:** `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆dimensions`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆level_count`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆level`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆cell_count`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆root`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆parent`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆child`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆depth`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆cell_min`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆cell_max`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆locate`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆refine`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆subdivide`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆refine_region`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆support_min`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆support_max`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆is_active`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆active_indices`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆basis_size`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆coefficient`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆set_coefficient`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆truncated_basis`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆fit_level`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆fit_level_penalized`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆fit`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆fit_penalized`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆evaluate`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆evaluate_points`, `dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆evaluate_grid`.
⍝
⍝ Handles are `('TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline' id)`; release them with `dm∆Delete obj`.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ obj←dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆new base
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ Starts a hierarchy whose level 0 (and root cell / domain) is `base`.
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `base` TensorBSpline handle: Level-0 spline; every axis basis must be clamped.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline handle
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: If an axis basis is not clamped or d > 20. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `datamunge::fda::TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline::TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline(TensorBSpline base)`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(¯2 0 3068) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline∆dimensions H;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Number of dimensions d.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
