∇Z←dm∆newton_interpolate B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ The same unique interpolating polynomial as lagrange_interpolate(), built instead via Newton's divided differences (an O(n^2) triangular table, then expanded into standard-basis coefficients). Included as the classical alternative construction: useful when points are added incrementally, since the divided-difference table extends by one row per new point rather than being rebuilt from scratch.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆newton_interpolate xs ys
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `xs` numeric vector
⍝ - `ys` numeric vector
⍝
⍝ **Result:** Polynomial handle
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: if xs/ys sizes disagree, are empty, or xs has a duplicate. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `Polynomial datamunge::algebra::newton_interpolate(const std::vector<double> & xs, const std::vector<double> & ys)`
