∇Z←dm∆leapfrog_gaussian_acceptance B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Exact expected Metropolis acceptance of one HMC proposal (fixed step size and number of leapfrog steps, unit mass) on a one-dimensional
⍝
⍝ `N(m,s^2)` target, started
⍝ in equilibrium.
⍝
⍝ In scaled coordinates `x=((q-m)/s,\,p)` each leapfrog step is the linear map
⍝ `M=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\-h/2&1\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}1&h\\0&1\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\-h/2&1\end{pmatrix}`
⍝ with `h=\varepsilon/s`, `\det M=1` and `\operatorname{tr}M=2-h^2`, so it is stable
⍝ exactly when `\varepsilon<2s`. Writing `x_0=r(\cos\phi,\sin\phi)` the energy error is
⍝ `\Delta H=r^2f(\phi)` with `f=\tfrac12(|M^L(\cos\phi,\sin\phi)|^2-1)`, and because
⍝ `r^2/2\sim\text{Exp}(1)` the radial average of `\min(1,e^{-\Delta H})` is
⍝ `1/(1+2f)` for `f>0` and 1 otherwise. The angle is integrated numerically.
⍝
⍝ `\varepsilon>0`.
⍝
⍝ `L\geq1`.
⍝
⍝ `s>0`.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆leapfrog_gaussian_acceptance step_size num_steps sd
⍝ Z←dm∆leapfrog_gaussian_acceptance step_size num_steps sd angles
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `step_size` number: Leapfrog step size
⍝ - `num_steps` integer: Number of leapfrog steps
⍝ - `sd` number: Target standard deviation
⍝ - `angles` integer, default 4096: Number of quadrature angles (at least 8).
⍝
⍝ **Result:** number: The expected acceptance probability.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: for non-positive arguments. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `double datamunge::bayes::leapfrog_gaussian_acceptance(double step_size, std::size_t num_steps, double sd, std::size_t angles = 4096)`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(58 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆gaussian_importance_efficiency B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Asymptotic effective-sample-size fraction
⍝
⍝ `N_{\text{eff}}/N` of importance sampling
⍝ a `d`-dimensional Gaussian target with a Gaussian proposal of the same mean and
⍝ covariance scaled by `k^2`.
⍝
⍝ `N_{\text{eff}}/N\to1/E_q[w^2]` for weights `w=p/q` normalised to mean 1. In one
⍝ dimension `E_q[w^2]=\int p^2/q=k^2/\sqrt{2k^2-1}`, finite only for `k^2>1/2`, and the
⍝ dimensions multiply, giving `\bigl(\sqrt{2k^2-1}/k^2\bigr)^d`. It is 1 at `k=1` and
⍝ 0 (infinite weight variance) for `k\le1/\sqrt2`.
⍝
⍝ `k>0`.
⍝
⍝ `d\ge1`.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆gaussian_importance_efficiency scale dimension
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `scale` number: Proposal scale factor
⍝ - `dimension` integer: Dimension
⍝
⍝ **Result:** number: The efficiency in [0, 1].
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: for non-positive `scale` or `dimension`. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `double datamunge::bayes::gaussian_importance_efficiency(double scale, int dimension)`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(59 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
