∇Z←dm∆laplacian_pyramid B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ The Laplacian pyramid: for every level except the last, level_i = (Gaussian level i) minus (Gaussian level i+1 upsampled back to level i's size) -- the detail lost by blurring+downsampling at that scale, re-centered at 128 (see detail::signed_difference_image()) so it stores as an ordinary image; the final level is the smallest Gaussian level kept as-is (the residual low-frequency image the whole pyramid was built from). Reconstructing the original from a Laplacian pyramid is the classical use (progressively add each un-recentered level back onto the upsampled reconstruction-so-far) but isn't implemented here -- this module only needed the decomposition direction.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆laplacian_pyramid img levels
⍝ Z←dm∆laplacian_pyramid img levels sigma
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `img` Image handle
⍝ - `levels` integer
⍝ - `sigma` number, default 1.0
⍝
⍝ **Result:** vector of (Image handle)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `std::vector<image::Image> datamunge::cv::laplacian_pyramid(const image::Image & img, int levels, double sigma = 1.0)`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(104 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆connected_components B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Labels every foreground (luma >= 128) connected region of `img` with a distinct positive integer via flood fill (simple breadth-first flood fill per unvisited foreground pixel, rather than the classical two-pass union-find labeling algorithm -- same asymptotic complexity, much easier to verify correct, matching this module's established simplicity-over-cleverness bias). `eight_connected` selects 8- neighborhood (diagonals count) vs. 4-neighborhood (orthogonal only) connectivity.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆connected_components img
⍝ Z←dm∆connected_components img eight_connected
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `img` Image handle
⍝ - `eight_connected` boolean (0 or 1), default 1
⍝
⍝ **Result:** LabelMap handle
⍝
⍝ - C++: `LabelMap datamunge::cv::connected_components(const image::Image & img, bool eight_connected = true)`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(105 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆otsu_threshold B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Otsu's method: the grayscale threshold (0-255) that maximizes the between-class variance of the pixels it would split into "below" and "at-or-above" groups -- the standard automatic threshold selection when a fixed threshold isn't known in advance. Pairs directly with image::threshold(img, otsu_threshold(img)).
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆otsu_threshold img
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `img` Image handle
⍝
⍝ **Result:** integer
⍝
⍝ - C++: `int datamunge::cv::otsu_threshold(const image::Image & img)`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(106 0) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆kmeans_segment B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ K-means color segmentation: clusters every pixel of `img` by its RGB value into `k` clusters (Lloyd's algorithm, deterministic fixed-seed initialization by sampling k distinct pixels) and returns a new RGB image where every pixel is replaced by its assigned cluster's mean color. Throws std::invalid_argument if `k` exceeds the number of distinct pixels available to seed from.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆kmeans_segment img k
⍝ Z←dm∆kmeans_segment img k max_iterations
⍝ Z←dm∆kmeans_segment img k max_iterations seed
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `img` Image handle
⍝ - `k` integer
⍝ - `max_iterations` integer, default 20
⍝ - `seed` integer, default 42
⍝
⍝ **Result:** Image handle
⍝
⍝ - C++: `image::Image datamunge::cv::kmeans_segment(const image::Image & img, int k, int max_iterations = 20, unsigned int seed = 42)`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(107 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆data_home;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ The cache root where downloaded datasets are stored.
⍝
⍝ The first of: `$DATAMUNGE_DATA_DIR`; `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/datamunge/datasets`;
⍝ `$HOME/.cache/datamunge/datasets`; on Windows `%LOCALAPPDATA%/datamunge/datasets`;
⍝ otherwise `build/datasets` relative to the working directory. Nothing is created.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
