∇Z←dm∆squared_distance B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Evaluate squared Euclidean distance using the original double arithmetic. No finite/range validation. Differences, products and sum can overflow, underflow or lose low bits; NaNs propagate according to floating arithmetic.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ std::cout << dm::geometry::squared_distance({0,0},{3,4}); // 25
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆squared_distance a b
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `a` Point2D handle: First point.
⍝ - `b` Point2D handle: Second point.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** number: Rounded squared distance, possibly infinity/NaN or underflowed zero.
⍝
⍝ - C++: `double datamunge::geometry::squared_distance(const Point2D & a, const Point2D & b)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆distance()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(113 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆distance B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Return sqrt(squared_distance(a,b)) with its original range limitations. A finite mathematical distance may overflow through the squared intermediate; this convenience primitive performs no checked hypot or input validation.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ std::cout << dm::geometry::distance({0,0},{3,4}); // 5
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆distance a b
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `a` Point2D handle: First point.
⍝ - `b` Point2D handle: Second point.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** number: Rounded square root of the double-valued squared distance.
⍝
⍝ - C++: `double datamunge::geometry::distance(const Point2D & a, const Point2D & b)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆squared_distance()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(114 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆cross B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Evaluate the original double determinant (b-a) cross (c-a). A positive exact determinant means counterclockwise, but this approximation can cancel, overflow or underflow and its returned sign is not certified. No tolerance or finite validation is applied. orientation_sign() supplies an exact sign for finite stored binary64 points; this primitive remains unchanged.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ std::cout << dm::geometry::cross({0,0},{1,0},{0,1}); // 1
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆cross a b c
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `a` Point2D handle: First point.
⍝ - `b` Point2D handle: Second point.
⍝ - `c` Point2D handle: Third point.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** number: Rounded double determinant, possibly infinity/NaN or a misleading zero.
⍝
⍝ - C++: `double datamunge::geometry::cross(const Point2D & a, const Point2D & b, const Point2D & c)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆orientation_sign()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(115 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆delaunay_triangulation B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Construct a complete unconstrained Delaunay triangulation with exact signs. Retains the original one-vector signature and Triangle layout. Finite coordinate validation precedes even trivial results. Duplicates coalesce to earliest ids; collinear/fewer-than-three distinct sites return no faces. Uses Bowyer-Watson cavity replacement with symbolic hull ghosts, avoiding any finite super-triangle whose proximity could remove valid narrow faces. All orientation/incircle signs are exact for finite stored binary64, including subnormals/extreme scales. Cocircular ties retain existing faces under the ordering in DelaunayTrace. Faces are strictly CCW, contain no ghosts and reference original ids. Finite output order is canonical as documented on DelaunayTrace. No boundary constraints, holes, Steiner refinement or minimum-angle guarantee is supplied. Input unchanged; O(n log(n+1)+u^2 log(u+1)) worst-case work for n records/u distinct sites with ordered cavity maps and full live scans; O(n+u) working/output storage, no shared state or optional resource caps. Exact-predicate cost is bounded per binary64 query.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ std::print("{}\n",dm::geometry::delaunay_triangulation({{0,0},{1,0},{.5,.000001}}).size()); // 1
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - A. Bowyer (1981), Computing Dirichlet tessellations, Computer Journal 24(2),162–166, doi:10.1093/comjnl/24.2.162.
⍝ - D. F. Watson (1981), Computing the n-dimensional Delaunay tessellation with application to Voronoi polytopes, Computer Journal 24(2),167–172, doi:10.1093/comjnl/24.2.167. This variant uses symbolic boundary faces, exact decoded-dyadic signs, earliest duplicate identities and ordered cavity edges.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆delaunay_triangulation points
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `points` vector of (Point2D handle): Borrowed finite planar sites, possibly empty/duplicate/collinear.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** vector of (Triangle handle): Owned complete finite mesh of the distinct sites' convex hull, or no faces for collinearity.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Any nonfinite coordinate, even on short input. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝ - LENGTH ERROR `dm: length_error: …`: Counter arithmetic or container capacity exceeded. (C++ `std::length_error`)
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: native: …`: Internal cavity topology invariant fails; no partial mesh returned. (C++ `std::logic_error`)
⍝ - WS FULL `dm: allocation: …`: Allocation fails. (C++ `std::bad_alloc`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `std::vector<Triangle> datamunge::geometry::delaunay_triangulation(const std::vector<Point2D> & points)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆delaunay_trace()`, `dm∆incircle_sign()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(116 0) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆delaunay_trace B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Capture actual symbolic-boundary cavity transitions from the same solver. No replay. Full finite validation precedes the original-record cap and all allocation. max_tests bounds cumulative incircle plus hull-side calls, checked before each complete live-face scan. Trace entries charge one per insertion plus removed faces, boundary edges and added faces, including ghosts, checked before retaining the event. Points/maps/final output and temporary cavities are excluded from that entry count; caps do not bound bytes or wall time. Zero disables only an optional cap. O(sum cavity/event sizes) additional history, at worst O(u^2), beyond the ordinary solver. Failure returns no partial result.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto t=dm::geometry::delaunay_trace({{0,0},{4,0},{4,4},{0,4},{2,2}},5,10);
⍝ std::print("{} {}\n",t.triangles.size(),t.work.incircle_tests+t.work.hull_side_tests); // 4 10
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - Bowyer (1981), doi:10.1093/comjnl/24.2.162; Watson (1981), doi:10.1093/comjnl/24.2.167; records the symbolic-boundary variant rather than a geometric replay.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆delaunay_trace points
⍝ Z←dm∆delaunay_trace points max_points
⍝ Z←dm∆delaunay_trace points max_points max_tests
⍝ Z←dm∆delaunay_trace points max_points max_tests max_entries
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `points` vector of (Point2D handle): Finite original coordinates, unchanged.
⍝ - `max_points` integer, default 1000000: Complete original-record cap, default 1000000; zero disables.
⍝ - `max_tests` integer, default 10000000: Cumulative complete-scan conflict cap, default 10000000; zero disables.
⍝ - `max_entries` integer, default 1000000: Cumulative event-entry cap, default 1000000; zero disables.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** DelaunayTrace handle: Owned points, original-id maps, finite mesh, actual events and work.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Any nonfinite coordinate. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝ - LENGTH ERROR `dm: length_error: …`: Optional cap, count arithmetic or container capacity exceeded. (C++ `std::length_error`)
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: native: …`: Internal cavity invariant fails. (C++ `std::logic_error`)
⍝ - WS FULL `dm: allocation: …`: Allocation fails. (C++ `std::bad_alloc`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `DelaunayTrace datamunge::geometry::delaunay_trace(const std::vector<Point2D> & points, std::size_t max_points = 1000000, std::size_t max_tests = 10000000, std::size_t max_entries = 1000000)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆DelaunayTrace`, `dm∆delaunay_triangulation()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(117 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆delaunay_analyze B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Measure a supplied mesh and test every nonvertex site against every circle. Performs the structural checks on DelaunayAnalysis first. The complete prospective circle count faces*(unique_sites-3) is checked before circle queries; zero cap disables only that limit. O(n log(n+1)+f log(f+1)+fu) work and O(n+f) storage; minimum angles are floating diagnostics and are not certified bounds. This is a local property analysis, not a global embedding/coverage certificate.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const std::vector<dm::geometry::Point2D> p{{0,0},{1,0},{1,1},{0,1}};
⍝ const auto a=dm::geometry::delaunay_analyze(p,dm::geometry::delaunay_triangulation(p));
⍝ std::print("{} {} {}\n",a.edges,a.boundary_edges,a.circle_tests); // 5 4 2
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆delaunay_analyze points triangles
⍝ Z←dm∆delaunay_analyze points triangles max_tests
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `points` vector of (Point2D handle): Finite original sites, unchanged.
⍝ - `triangles` vector of (Triangle handle): Finite CCW faces with valid original indices, unchanged.
⍝ - `max_tests` integer, default 1000000: Complete exact-circle query cap, default 1000000; zero disables.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** DelaunayAnalysis handle: Owned scalar topology, actual query/violation counts and approximate angle.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Nonfinite coordinates, bad/degenerate/non-CCW/duplicate faces or nonmanifold edges. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝ - LENGTH ERROR `dm: length_error: …`: Circle-count arithmetic or optional cap exceeded. (C++ `std::length_error`)
⍝ - WS FULL `dm: allocation: …`: Allocation fails. (C++ `std::bad_alloc`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `DelaunayAnalysis datamunge::geometry::delaunay_analyze(const std::vector<Point2D> & points, const std::vector<Triangle> & triangles, std::size_t max_tests = 1000000)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆DelaunayAnalysis`, `dm∆delaunay_triangulation()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(118 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆delaunay_uniform_cloud B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Generate the report's seeded uniform square-cloud control in the core. Uses mt19937(seed) and uniform_real_distribution<double>(0,extent), x then y for each point. Same-library runs reproduce the original report's seed20250728 and 30-site square when defaults are used. Standard distribution algorithms need not match across C++ libraries, so the seed is not a universal byte fixture. Coincident draws are allowed and later triangulation deduplicates them. O(count) work/storage, owned output, no global RNG or shared mutation.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ std::print("{}\n",dm::geometry::delaunay_uniform_cloud(30).size()); // 30
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆delaunay_uniform_cloud count
⍝ Z←dm∆delaunay_uniform_cloud count seed
⍝ Z←dm∆delaunay_uniform_cloud count seed extent
⍝ Z←dm∆delaunay_uniform_cloud count seed extent max_points
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `count` integer: Number of original generated records, zero allowed.
⍝ - `seed` integer, default 20250728: mt19937 seed, default 20250728.
⍝ - `extent` number, default 10.0: Positive finite square extent, default 10; checked even for count zero.
⍝ - `max_points` integer, default 1000000: Optional complete output-record cap, default 1000000; zero disables.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** vector of (Point2D handle): Owned generated points in [0,extent) on each axis.
