 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆dynamic_time_warping_work B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Compute unrestricted absolute-loss DTW and actual logical work without cost history. Owns the result; borrows finite inputs without mutation. No band, slope bound, step weighting, length normalization or fitted parameters. Finite ties choose diagonal, then advance_a, then advance_b. O(n*m) recurrence time, O(n*m+n+m) scratch/output, with only two double cost rows and n*m predecessor bytes. Complete input/geometry validation precedes allocation. Positive overflowing off-path costs remain infinity; only a nonfinite final optimum is rejected. Under ordinary double arithmetic the returned witness sums to the returned distance in forward order. Concurrent independent calls share no mutable state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ See DTWWork and DTWComputation for executed work examples.
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - H. Sakoe and S. Chiba (1978), Dynamic programming algorithm optimization for
⍝   spoken word recognition, IEEE TASSP26(1):43-49. doi:10.1109/TASSP.1978.1163055.
⍝   This is the unrestricted scalar absolute-loss variant, without the paper's window/slope rules.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆dynamic_time_warping_work a b
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `a` numeric vector: Nonempty finite scalar sequence, length at most INT_MAX.
⍝ - `b` numeric vector: Nonempty finite scalar sequence, length at most INT_MAX.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** DTWComputation handle: Independent optimal result and counts, with no retained input/cost history.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: For empty or nonfinite input. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝ - LENGTH ERROR `dm: length_error: …`: For geometry/index/container bounds. (C++ `std::length_error`)
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: arithmetic_error: …`: If the final minimum is not finite double. (C++ `std::overflow_error`)
⍝ - WS FULL `dm: allocation: …`: If scratch or result storage cannot be allocated. (C++ `std::bad_alloc`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `DTWComputation datamunge::geometry::dynamic_time_warping_work(const std::vector<double> & a, const std::vector<double> & b)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆dynamic_time_warping()`, `dm∆dynamic_time_warping_trace()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(134 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆dynamic_time_warping B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Preserve the original two-argument unrestricted absolute-loss DTW entry point. Same validation, ownership, deterministic ties, numerical limits and complexity as dynamic_time_warping_work(); discards only its work counters. This distance is not a metric: unequal stretched sequences can have zero cost and the triangle inequality can fail. Equal-length diagonal alignment bounds it by rigid L1 loss.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ See DTWResult for the executed original stretched-sequence example.
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - H. Sakoe and S. Chiba (1978), IEEE TASSP26(1):43-49, doi:10.1109/TASSP.1978.1163055.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆dynamic_time_warping a b
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `a` numeric vector: Nonempty finite first sequence, length at most INT_MAX.
⍝ - `b` numeric vector: Nonempty finite second sequence, length at most INT_MAX.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** DTWResult handle: Owned optimal distance and inclusive zero-based parallel int paths.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: For empty or nonfinite input. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝ - LENGTH ERROR `dm: length_error: …`: For geometry/index/container bounds. (C++ `std::length_error`)
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: arithmetic_error: …`: If the final optimum is not finite double. (C++ `std::overflow_error`)
⍝ - WS FULL `dm: allocation: …`: If scratch or result storage cannot be allocated. (C++ `std::bad_alloc`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `DTWResult datamunge::geometry::dynamic_time_warping(const std::vector<double> & a, const std::vector<double> & b)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆dynamic_time_warping_work()`, `dm∆dtw_path_cost()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(135 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆dynamic_time_warping_trace B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Capture actual positive-cell costs and predecessor choices from the shared DTW kernel. Validates complete finite inputs/geometry before checking the optional complete capture budget, then copies inputs and runs once. Owns every snapshot. No partial trace/result escapes on failure. Same variant/ties/numerical rules as ordinary DTW.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ See DTWTrace and DTWPredecessor for executed capture examples.
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - H. Sakoe and S. Chiba (1978), IEEE TASSP26(1):43-49, doi:10.1109/TASSP.1978.1163055.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆dynamic_time_warping_trace a b
⍝ Z←dm∆dynamic_time_warping_trace a b max_entries
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `a` numeric vector: Nonempty finite first input, length at most INT_MAX.
⍝ - `b` numeric vector: Nonempty finite second input, length at most INT_MAX.
⍝ - `max_entries` integer, default 1000000: Complete logical entry cap, default 1000000; zero disables only cap.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** DTWTrace handle: Independent input, costs, directions, optimal result, counts and capture cost.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: For empty or nonfinite input, before budget checks. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝ - LENGTH ERROR `dm: length_error: …`: For geometry/index/container/capture limits. (C++ `std::length_error`)
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: arithmetic_error: …`: If the final optimum is not finite double. (C++ `std::overflow_error`)
⍝ - WS FULL `dm: allocation: …`: If owned or working storage cannot be allocated. (C++ `std::bad_alloc`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `DTWTrace datamunge::geometry::dynamic_time_warping_trace(const std::vector<double> & a, const std::vector<double> & b, std::size_t max_entries = 1000000)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆dtw_trace_entries()`, `dm∆dynamic_time_warping_work()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(136 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆dtw_path_cost B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Validate a complete monotone witness and sum its unshifted absolute losses. Checks all finite input and path geometry before numerical accumulation: equal nonempty path lengths, in-bounds indices, exact endpoints and unit advances (1,0), (0,1), (1,1). This certifies feasibility/cost, not optimality. O(n+m+L) validation/sum, no allocations, input unchanged; no shared mutable state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto r=dm::geometry::dynamic_time_warping({1,2},{1,2});
⍝ std::print("{}\n",dm::geometry::dtw_path_cost({1,2},{1,2},r.path_a,r.path_b)); // 0
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆dtw_path_cost a b path_a path_b
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `a` numeric vector: Nonempty finite first sequence.
⍝ - `b` numeric vector: Nonempty finite second sequence.
⍝ - `path_a` integer vector: Borrowed signed first indices, zero through a.size()-1.
⍝ - `path_b` integer vector: Borrowed parallel signed second indices, zero through b.size()-1.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** number: Finite forward-order sum of absolute differences on the supplied witness.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: For invalid finite inputs or path shape/indices/steps/endpoints. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝ - LENGTH ERROR `dm: length_error: …`: For input index/geometry bounds. (C++ `std::length_error`)
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: arithmetic_error: …`: If the supplied path's accumulated cost is not finite double. (C++ `std::overflow_error`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `double datamunge::geometry::dtw_path_cost(const std::vector<double> & a, const std::vector<double> & b, const std::vector<int> & path_a, const std::vector<int> & path_b)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆DTWResult`, `dm∆plot_dtw_alignment()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(137 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆plot_dtw_alignment B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Draw two scalar series and their validated DTW correspondence segments natively. Costs use unshifted inputs; only drawing adds offset to a and subtracts it from b. Requires result.distance to equal the validated forward witness sum exactly; it does not independently prove optimality. Validates complete inputs/witness, finite plotted coordinates and optional path-pair cap before drawing allocation. Owns all series; no file I/O or model mutation. Concurrent calls share no state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto r=dm::geometry::dynamic_time_warping({1,2},{1,2});
⍝ const auto p=dm::geometry::plot_dtw_alignment({1,2},{1,2},r);
⍝ std::print("{}\n",p.series().size()); // 3
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆plot_dtw_alignment a b result
⍝ Z←dm∆plot_dtw_alignment a b result offset
⍝ Z←dm∆plot_dtw_alignment a b result offset max_pairs
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `a` numeric vector: Nonempty finite first sequence.
⍝ - `b` numeric vector: Nonempty finite second sequence.
⍝ - `result` DTWResult handle: Borrowed complete feasible result with exact finite witness cost.
⍝ - `offset` number, default 1.6000000000000001: Finite nonnegative vertical display separation per series, default 1.6.
⍝ - `max_pairs` integer, default 4096: Optional complete correspondence cap, default 4096; zero disables only cap.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** RPlot handle: Editable owned RPlot with two lines and gray correspondence segments.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: For invalid inputs/witness/distance/offset. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝ - LENGTH ERROR `dm: length_error: …`: For input geometry/index limits or exceeded nonzero pair cap. (C++ `std::length_error`)
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: arithmetic_error: …`: For witness cost or shifted drawing-coordinate overflow. (C++ `std::overflow_error`)
⍝ - WS FULL `dm: allocation: …`: If owned plot storage cannot be allocated. (C++ `std::bad_alloc`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `graphics::RPlot datamunge::geometry::plot_dtw_alignment(const std::vector<double> & a, const std::vector<double> & b, const DTWResult & result, double offset = 1.6000000000000001, std::size_t max_pairs = 4096)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆dtw_path_cost()`, `dm∆RPlot`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(138 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆discrete_frechet_distance B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ The discrete Fréchet distance between two point sequences ("curves") p and q (Eiter & Mannila, 1994): the minimum, over every monotone coupling between the two curves, of the MAXIMUM pairwise distance along that coupling -- informally, the shortest leash length needed for a person walking along p and a dog walking along q (both only moving forward, never backward) to stay connected the whole way. Computed via the standard O(n*m) dynamic program (bottom-up, not the naive exponential recursion). Differs from DTW in exactly one respect: DTW sums (cumulative cost) where Fréchet takes a max (bottleneck cost) -- Fréchet cares about the worst single moment of divergence, DTW about total accumulated divergence.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆discrete_frechet_distance p q
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `p` vector of (Point2D handle)
⍝ - `q` vector of (Point2D handle)
⍝
⍝ **Result:** number
⍝
⍝ - C++: `double datamunge::geometry::discrete_frechet_distance(const std::vector<Point2D> & p, const std::vector<Point2D> & q)`
