∇Z←dm∆triangulate_polygon B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Triangulates a SIMPLE polygon (convex or not, but not self-intersecting) by ear clipping: repeatedly finds a "ear" -- a convex vertex whose neighbor-triangle contains no other polygon vertex -- clips it off as one output triangle, and repeats on the remaining (n-1)-vertex polygon. O(n^3) worst case (each of the O(n) clipped ears requires an O(n) scan to verify, each vertex check O(1)) -- unlike delaunay_triangulation() (which only sees an unordered point SET), this respects the polygon's actual boundary, so it correctly handles concave (non-convex) shapes. Every triangle's vertices are indices into `polygon`. Works for any winding order (detects and internally corrects clockwise input). Returns empty for fewer than 3 vertices; a triangle input returns itself unchanged.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆triangulate_polygon polygon
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `polygon` vector of (Point2D handle)
⍝
⍝ **Result:** vector of (Triangle handle)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `std::vector<Triangle> datamunge::geometry::triangulate_polygon(const std::vector<Point2D> & polygon)`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(158 0) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
