∇dm∆write_png B;⍙M;⍙E;⍙Z
⍝ Writes `img` as an 8-bit-depth, non-interlaced PNG, using its ImageMode directly as the PNG color type (Grayscale/GrayscaleAlpha/RGB/RGBA all have a direct PNG equivalent, so no conversion is ever needed). Every scanline is written with filter type 0 (None) -- simpler and always correct, at the cost of a somewhat larger file than a filter-optimizing encoder would produce; any standard PNG decoder still reads the result correctly.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ dm∆write_png img path
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `img` Image handle
⍝ - `path` character vector
⍝
⍝ - C++: `void datamunge::image::write_png(const Image & img, const std::string & path)`
 (⍙M ⍙E ⍙Z)←(180 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆expected_improvement B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Expected improvement of a Gaussian prediction below an incumbent (minimisation).
⍝
⍝ For `Y\sim N(\mu,\sigma^2)` and target `t=\text{incumbent}-\xi`,
⍝ `\operatorname{E}\max(t-Y,0)=(t-\mu)\Phi(u)+\sigma\varphi(u)` with `u=(t-\mu)/\sigma`.
⍝
⍝ `\mu`.
⍝
⍝ `\sigma\ge0`; 0 gives `\max(t-\mu,0)`.
⍝
⍝ `\xi\ge0` an improvement must exceed.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ std::print("{:.4f}\n", dm::optim::expected_improvement(0.0, 1.0, 0.0));  // 0.3989 = phi(0)
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆expected_improvement mean sd incumbent
⍝ Z←dm∆expected_improvement mean sd incumbent exploration
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `mean` number: Predicted mean
⍝ - `sd` number: Predicted standard deviation
⍝ - `incumbent` number: Smallest value observed so far.
⍝ - `exploration` number, default 0.0: Margin
⍝
⍝ **Result:** number: The expected improvement (never negative).
⍝
⍝ - C++: `double datamunge::optim::expected_improvement(double mean, double sd, double incumbent, double exploration = 0.0)`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(181 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆probability_of_improvement B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Probability that a Gaussian prediction falls below incumbent − exploration.
⍝
⍝ `\mu`.
⍝
⍝ `\xi\ge0`.
⍝
⍝ `\Phi\big((\text{incumbent}-\xi-\mu)/\sigma\big)`.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ std::print("{:.4f}\n", dm::optim::probability_of_improvement(1.0, 1.0, 0.0));  // 0.1587
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆probability_of_improvement mean sd incumbent
⍝ Z←dm∆probability_of_improvement mean sd incumbent exploration
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `mean` number: Predicted mean
⍝ - `sd` number: Predicted standard deviation; 0 gives 0 or 1.
⍝ - `incumbent` number: Smallest value observed so far.
⍝ - `exploration` number, default 0.0: Margin
⍝
⍝ **Result:** number
⍝
⍝ - C++: `double datamunge::optim::probability_of_improvement(double mean, double sd, double incumbent, double exploration = 0.0)`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(182 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆one_way_anova B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Classical equal-variance one-way ANOVA, like R oneway.test(var.equal=TRUE).
⍝
⍝ Tests equality of group means with between/within mean-square ratio. Independent normal
⍝ observations and a common within-group population variance give the exact F reference. This is
⍝ not Welch ANOVA. O(N) work, O(1) auxiliary storage; input unchanged, no omission or shared
⍝ mutable state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto a=dm::stats::one_way_anova({1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9},{3,3,3});
⍝ std::print("{:.0f}\n",a.statistic); // 27
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/oneway.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆one_way_anova values group_sizes
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `values` numeric vector: Finite observations concatenated group by group.
⍝ - `group_sizes` integer vector: At least two positive sizes summing exactly to values.size(); N must exceed the group count.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: statistic=F, parameter1=k-1, parameter2=N-k and upper-tail p_value; no CI or group estimates.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Invalid grouping/nonfinite input, no residual df, zero within- group variation or arithmetic overflow. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::one_way_anova(const std::vector<double> & values, const std::vector<std::size_t> & group_sizes)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(183 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆kruskal_wallis_test B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Tie-corrected Kruskal-Wallis rank test, like R kruskal.test().
⍝
⍝ Ranks the pooled observations with average ties and uses the chi-square(k-1) approximation.
⍝ The usual exchangeability null is identical independent group distributions; a common-shape
⍝ shift model permits a location interpretation. It is not an omnibus test for every possible
⍝ distributional difference. Small groups can invalidate the approximation. O(N log N) work/O(N)
⍝ space, no mutation, omission or shared state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto h=dm::stats::kruskal_wallis_test({1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9},{3,3,3});
⍝ std::print("{:.1f}\n",h.statistic); // 7.2
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/kruskal.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆kruskal_wallis_test values group_sizes
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `values` numeric vector: Finite observations concatenated by group.
⍝ - `group_sizes` integer vector: At least two positive group sizes summing to values.size().
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: statistic=H, parameter1=k-1 and upper-tail approximate p_value; no CI.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Invalid groups, nonfinite input or all observations tied. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::kruskal_wallis_test(const std::vector<double> & values, const std::vector<std::size_t> & group_sizes)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(184 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆chi_squared_goodness_of_fit B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Pearson chi-square test of specified category probabilities, like R chisq.test().
⍝
⍝ Uses sum((observed-expected)^2/expected) and chi-square(k-1); the probabilities are fixed, not
⍝ estimated here. Expected counts must be sufficiently large for the approximation. Nonnegative
⍝ fractional counts are accepted algebraically but do not automatically define a sampling model.
⍝ O(k) work/storage, inputs unchanged, no shared state or omission.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto q=dm::stats::chi_squared_goodness_of_fit({10,10});
⍝ std::print("{:.0f}\n",q.statistic); // 0
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/chisq.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆chi_squared_goodness_of_fit observed
⍝ Z←dm∆chi_squared_goodness_of_fit observed expected_probabilities
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `observed` numeric vector: At least two finite nonnegative counts with positive finite total.
⍝ - `expected_probabilities` numeric vector, default the C++ default: Empty (default) means uniform; otherwise positive finite probabilities matching counts and summing to one within 1e-10.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: statistic=X-squared, parameter1=k-1 and approximate upper-tail p_value; no CI.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Invalid counts/probabilities, sizes or zero/nonfinite total. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::chi_squared_goodness_of_fit(const std::vector<double> & observed, const std::vector<double> & expected_probabilities = {})`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(185 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆chi_squared_test_independence B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Pearson chi-square test for a contingency table, like R chisq.test().
⍝
⍝ Expected counts are row_sum*column_sum/total; df=(rows-1)(columns-1). The asymptotic reference
⍝ assumes independent multinomial/product-multinomial observations and adequately populated
⍝ expected cells. O(rows*columns) work and O(rows+columns) space; inputs unchanged, no shared
⍝ state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto q=dm::stats::chi_squared_test_independence({10,10,10,10},2,2);
⍝ std::print("{:.0f}\n",q.statistic); // 0
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/chisq.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆chi_squared_test_independence table nrows ncols
⍝ Z←dm∆chi_squared_test_independence table nrows ncols correct
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `table` numeric vector: Finite nonnegative row-major counts with strictly positive margins; integer counts are the usual sampling model.
⍝ - `nrows` integer: At least two rows.
⍝ - `ncols` integer: At least two columns; table length must equal nrows*ncols.
⍝ - `correct` boolean (0 or 1), default 1: True (default) subtracts min(.5,abs(O-E)) from each absolute residual only for 2x2 tables.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: statistic=X-squared, parameter1=df, approximate upper-tail p_value; no CI.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Invalid shape, counts/margins or nonfinite total. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::chi_squared_test_independence(const std::vector<double> & table, std::size_t nrows, std::size_t ncols, bool correct = true)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(186 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆pearson_correlation_test B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Pearson product-moment correlation test with Fisher-z confidence bounds.
⍝
⍝ Uses t=r*sqrt((n-2)/(1-r^2)). The exact zero-correlation null reference requires independent
⍝ bivariate-normal pairs; Fisher-z intervals are approximate. At n=3 no confidence interval is
⍝ reported; n>=4 gets one. Perfect correlations use infinite-statistic limits. O(n) work, no
⍝ input mutation/omission or shared state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto r=dm::stats::pearson_correlation_test({1,2,3,4},{2,4,6,8});
⍝ std::print("{:.1f}\n",r.estimate1); // 1.0
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/cor.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆pearson_correlation_test x y
⍝ Z←dm∆pearson_correlation_test x y alternative
⍝ Z←dm∆pearson_correlation_test x y alternative conf_level
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `x` numeric vector: At least three finite nonconstant first measurements.
⍝ - `y` numeric vector: Same-length finite nonconstant second measurements, paired by index.
⍝ - `alternative` integer (dm∆Alternative∆…, or the enumerator's name), default dm∆Alternative∆TwoSided: TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for population correlation.
⍝ - `conf_level` number, default 0.94999999999999996: Finite confidence level in (0,1), default .95.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: statistic=t, parameter1=n-2, estimate1=r and p_value; has_conf_int only for n>=4.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Invalid lengths, nonfinite/constant samples or invalid controls. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::pearson_correlation_test(const std::vector<double> & x, const std::vector<double> & y, Alternative alternative = TwoSided, double conf_level = 0.94999999999999996)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(187 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆spearman_correlation_test B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Spearman correlation with average ranks and a t-approximation p-value.
⍝
⍝ Computes Pearson correlation on ranks. The reference is always t(n-2), not a small-sample
⍝ exact permutation distribution; ties receive average ranks. Tests zero rank correlation under
⍝ the approximation, not arbitrary independence. O(n log n) work/O(n) storage, no
⍝ mutation/omission or shared state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto r=dm::stats::spearman_correlation_test({1,2,3,4},{10,20,30,40});
⍝ std::print("{:.1f}\n",r.estimate1); // 1.0
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/cor.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆spearman_correlation_test x y
⍝ Z←dm∆spearman_correlation_test x y alternative
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `x` numeric vector: At least three finite nonconstant first measurements.
⍝ - `y` numeric vector: Same-length finite nonconstant paired second measurements.
⍝ - `alternative` integer (dm∆Alternative∆…, or the enumerator's name), default dm∆Alternative∆TwoSided: TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for population rank correlation.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: statistic=rank-correlation t, parameter1=n-2, estimate1=rho, approximate p_value; no CI.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Invalid lengths, nonfinite/constant samples or alternative. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::spearman_correlation_test(const std::vector<double> & x, const std::vector<double> & y, Alternative alternative = TwoSided)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(188 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆kendall_tau B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Kendall rank coefficients by Knight inversion counting.
⍝
⍝ Sorts paired values and counts concordant, discordant and tied pairs in O(n log n) work/O(n)
⍝ storage. Inputs are unchanged and calls share no mutable state. This is a coefficient
⍝ calculation, not a hypothesis test; tau_b is zero when its denominator vanishes.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto k=dm::stats::kendall_tau({1,2,3,4},{1,2,3,4});
⍝ std::print("{}\n",k.concordant); // 6
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/cor.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆kendall_tau x y
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `x` numeric vector: At least two finite first coordinates.
⍝ - `y` numeric vector: Equal-length finite second coordinates, paired by index.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** KendallTau handle: Owned pair counts, tau_a and tie-adjusted tau_b.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Lengths differ, fewer than two values, or nonfinite observations. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `KendallTau datamunge::stats::kendall_tau(const std::vector<double> & x, const std::vector<double> & y)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(189 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆fisher_exact_test_2x2 B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Conditional Fisher exact test of a 2x2 table with fixed margins.
⍝
⍝ Conditions on row/column totals and tests odds ratio one. Two-sided p-values sum probabilities
⍝ no larger than the observed table (relative ordering tolerance 1e-7); they are not generally
⍝ twice the smaller tail. Direct tail summation avoids 1-CDF cancellation. Empty margins give
⍝ p=1. O(s) work/O(1) auxiliary storage for feasible support s; inputs unchanged, no shared
⍝ state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto f=dm::stats::fisher_exact_test_2x2(0,3,3,0);
⍝ std::print("{:.1f}\n",f.p_value); // 0.1
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/fisher.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆fisher_exact_test_2x2 a b c d
⍝ Z←dm∆fisher_exact_test_2x2 a b c d alternative
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `a` integer: Top-left nonnegative integer count.
⍝ - `b` integer: Top-right count.
⍝ - `c` integer: Bottom-left count.
⍝ - `d` integer: Bottom-right count; total must fit size_t.
⍝ - `alternative` integer (dm∆Alternative∆…, or the enumerator's name), default dm∆Alternative∆TwoSided: TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for the odds ratio relative to one.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: statistic=a, p_value and estimate1=sample odds ratio a*d/(b*c), not R conditional MLE; denominator zero gives infinity for positive numerator or NaN for 0/0. No confidence interval.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Count-total overflow or invalid alternative. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::fisher_exact_test_2x2(std::size_t a, std::size_t b, std::size_t c, std::size_t d, Alternative alternative = TwoSided)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(190 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆ks_test_one_sample_normal B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ One-sample KS test against a fully specified continuous normal CDF.
⍝
⍝ Two-sided p-values use the Stephens corrected asymptotic Kolmogorov reference; one-sided
⍝ values use exp(-2*n*D^2). Mean and sd must be fixed independently of this sample: fitting them
⍝ here invalidates the stated calibration. This is not a Lilliefors test. Ties are accepted, but
⍝ continuous-null calibration is affected by rounding. O(n log n) work/O(n) storage, input
⍝ unchanged, no omission/shared state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto k=dm::stats::ks_test_one_sample_normal({-1,0,1});
⍝ std::print("{:.0f}\n",k.parameter1); // 3
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/ks.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆ks_test_one_sample_normal x
⍝ Z←dm∆ks_test_one_sample_normal x mean
⍝ Z←dm∆ks_test_one_sample_normal x mean sd
⍝ Z←dm∆ks_test_one_sample_normal x mean sd alternative
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `x` numeric vector: At least two finite observations.
⍝ - `mean` number, default 0.0: Finite prespecified normal mean, default zero.
⍝ - `sd` number, default 1.0: Positive finite prespecified normal SD, default one.
⍝ - `alternative` integer (dm∆Alternative∆…, or the enumerator's name), default dm∆Alternative∆TwoSided: TwoSided (default); Greater tests empirical CDF above reference, Less below (opposite to a location alternative).
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: statistic=D or D+/D-, parameter1=n and approximate p_value; no CI.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Too few/nonfinite values, invalid mean/sd or alternative. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::ks_test_one_sample_normal(const std::vector<double> & x, double mean = 0.0, double sd = 1.0, Alternative alternative = TwoSided)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(191 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆ks_test_one_sample_uniform B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ One-sample KS test against a specified continuous uniform CDF.
⍝
⍝ Same asymptotic p-value and tie caveats as ks_test_one_sample_normal(); limits must be
⍝ prespecified rather than estimated from x. O(n log n) work/O(n) storage; no mutation, omission
⍝ or shared state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto k=dm::stats::ks_test_one_sample_uniform({.2,.4,.6,.8});
⍝ std::print("{:.1f}\n",k.statistic); // 0.2
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/ks.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆ks_test_one_sample_uniform x
⍝ Z←dm∆ks_test_one_sample_uniform x lower
⍝ Z←dm∆ks_test_one_sample_uniform x lower upper
⍝ Z←dm∆ks_test_one_sample_uniform x lower upper alternative
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `x` numeric vector: At least two finite observations.
⍝ - `lower` number, default 0.0: Finite prespecified lower bound, default zero.
⍝ - `upper` number, default 1.0: Finite upper bound greater than lower, default one.
⍝ - `alternative` integer (dm∆Alternative∆…, or the enumerator's name), default dm∆Alternative∆TwoSided: TwoSided (default), Greater for empirical CDF above reference or Less below.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: statistic=D or D+/D-, parameter1=n, approximate p_value; no CI.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Invalid sample, limits or alternative. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::ks_test_one_sample_uniform(const std::vector<double> & x, double lower = 0.0, double upper = 1.0, Alternative alternative = TwoSided)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(192 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆ks_test_two_sample B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Two-sample KS comparison of empirical CDFs with asymptotic p-values.
⍝
⍝ Uses effective n=n_x*n_y/(n_x+n_y) in the asymptotic reference. This is not an exact finite-
⍝ sample calculation; ties/rounding and very small groups affect calibration. O(N log N)
⍝ work/O(N) storage; inputs unchanged, no omission/shared state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto k=dm::stats::ks_test_two_sample({1,2,3},{1,2,3});
⍝ std::print("{:.0f}\n",k.statistic); // 0
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/ks.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆ks_test_two_sample x y
⍝ Z←dm∆ks_test_two_sample x y alternative
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `x` numeric vector: Nonempty finite first sample.
⍝ - `y` numeric vector: Nonempty finite independent second sample.
⍝ - `alternative` integer (dm∆Alternative∆…, or the enumerator's name), default dm∆Alternative∆TwoSided: TwoSided (default); Greater tests F_x above F_y, Less below, so Greater corresponds to stochastically smaller x.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: statistic=D or D+/D-, parameter1=n_x, parameter2=n_y and approximate p_value; no CI.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Empty/nonfinite samples or invalid alternative. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::ks_test_two_sample(const std::vector<double> & x, const std::vector<double> & y, Alternative alternative = TwoSided)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(193 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆shapiro_francia_test B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Shapiro-Francia composite normality test, like R nortest::sf.test().
⍝
⍝ Squared correlation of sorted observations with Blom normal scores, using Royston (1993) log-
⍝ normal p-value approximation. Tests some normal distribution; a large p-value does not prove
⍝ normality. Rounding/ties affect calibration. O(n log n) work/O(n) storage; input unchanged, no
⍝ omission/shared state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto s=dm::stats::shapiro_francia_test({1,2,3,4,5});
⍝ std::print("{}\n",s.has_conf_int); // false
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://search.r-project.org/CRAN/refmans/nortest/html/sf.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆shapiro_francia_test x
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `x` numeric vector: Between 5 and 5000 finite, nonconstant observations.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: statistic=estimate1=W-prime and approximate p_value; no CI.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Invalid sample size, nonfinite input or constant sample. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::shapiro_francia_test(const std::vector<double> & x)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(194 0) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆p_adjust B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Adjust a vector of p-values for multiple comparisons, matching R's p.adjust().
⍝
⍝ - Bonferroni: p[i] * n, capped at 1 -- simplest and most conservative FWER control.
⍝ - Holm: step-down Bonferroni (Holm-Bonferroni) -- uniformly more powerful than
⍝   plain Bonferroni while still controlling FWER, no extra assumptions.
⍝ - Hochberg: step-up FWER control -- more powerful than Holm, but assumes the test
⍝   statistics are independent (or positively dependent).
⍝ - Hommel: step-up FWER control, uniformly more powerful than Hochberg under the
⍝   same independence assumption; the most expensive to compute (O(n^2)). For n == 2
⍝   this degenerates to (and is computed as) Hochberg, matching R.
⍝ - BH (Benjamini-Hochberg, aka "fdr" in R): controls the false discovery rate under
⍝   independence or positive regression dependence -- the standard choice for large-
⍝   scale testing (e.g. genomics, many simultaneous hypothesis tests).
⍝ - BY (Benjamini-Yekutieli): controls the FDR under arbitrary dependence; more
⍝   conservative than BH.
⍝ - None: returns the input p-values unchanged.
⍝
⍝ As in R, a single p-value (n <= 1) is always returned unchanged regardless of
⍝ `method`.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆p_adjust p
⍝ Z←dm∆p_adjust p method
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `p` numeric vector
⍝ - `method` integer (dm∆PAdjustMethod∆…, or the enumerator's name), default dm∆PAdjustMethod∆BH
⍝
⍝ **Result:** numeric vector
⍝
⍝ - C++: `std::vector<double> datamunge::stats::p_adjust(const std::vector<double> & p, PAdjustMethod method = BH)`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(195 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆westfall_young_adjust B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Westfall-Young single-step minP adjustment (Westfall & Young, 1993) -- a resampling- based FWER correction that accounts for the actual dependence structure between tests (unlike the analytic methods above, which either assume independence or make no assumption at all).
⍝
⍝ `resampled_p` is a B x m matrix: row b holds the m p-values recomputed on the
⍝ b-th permutation or bootstrap resample (generated by the caller -- this function is
⍝ purely the combinatorial engine, not the resampling scheme itself). `sorted_p` is
⍝ the m original p-values sorted ascending, and column k of every row in `resampled_p`
⍝ must correspond to the same hypothesis as sorted_p[k] (i.e. the caller sorts once and
⍝ reorders both consistently before calling).
⍝
⍝ Single-step: every hypothesis is compared against the same reference distribution --
⍝ the per-resample minimum p-value taken over *all* m hypotheses -- so adjusted p[k] =
⍝ (fraction of resamples whose overall minimum p-value <= sorted_p[k]). This is
⍝ automatically monotonic in sorted_p and valid under subset pivotality, but less
⍝ powerful than the step-down refinement below since it never narrows the comparison
⍝ set as hypotheses are resolved.
⍝
⍝ Returned in the same (ascending sorted_p) order as the input; the caller is
⍝ responsible for mapping back to original hypothesis order if needed.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆westfall_young_adjust resampled_p sorted_p
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `resampled_p` numeric matrix (one row per element)
⍝ - `sorted_p` numeric vector
⍝
⍝ **Result:** numeric vector
⍝
⍝ - C++: `std::vector<double> datamunge::stats::westfall_young_adjust(const std::vector<std::vector<double>> & resampled_p, const std::vector<double> & sorted_p)`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(196 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆romano_wolf_adjust B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Romano-Wolf step-down resampling adjustment (Romano & Wolf, 2005) -- like westfall_young_adjust, but more powerful: at each step k (in ascending sorted_p order), the reference distribution is the per-resample minimum p-value taken only over the *remaining* hypotheses k..m-1, narrowing by one hypothesis at each step rather than always using all m. This is the step-down generalization that controls FWER without requiring subset pivotality. Monotonicity (adjusted p[k] >= adjusted p[k-1]) is enforced explicitly, since narrowing the comparison set at each step means it isn't automatic the way it is for the single-step procedure above.
⍝
⍝ Same input/output conventions as westfall_young_adjust: `resampled_p` is a B x m
⍝ matrix with column k aligned to sorted_p[k], and the result is returned in the same
⍝ sorted order.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆romano_wolf_adjust resampled_p sorted_p
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `resampled_p` numeric matrix (one row per element)
⍝ - `sorted_p` numeric vector
⍝
⍝ **Result:** numeric vector
⍝
⍝ - C++: `std::vector<double> datamunge::stats::romano_wolf_adjust(const std::vector<std::vector<double>> & resampled_p, const std::vector<double> & sorted_p)`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(197 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆proportion_test_one_sample B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ One-proportion score test with an uncorrected Wilson confidence interval.
⍝
⍝ Tests a fixed Bernoulli success probability using the signed null-standard-error z statistic;
⍝ statistic stores z^2 but the chosen tail uses its sign. The optional continuity correction
⍝ affects the test only: its Wilson interval is uncorrected and need not invert that test.
⍝ Approximation requires sufficiently populated success/failure counts. O(1) work/storage, no
⍝ shared state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto p=dm::stats::proportion_test_one_sample(45,100);
⍝ std::print("{:.2f}\n",p.estimate1); // 0.45
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/prop.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆proportion_test_one_sample successes n
⍝ Z←dm∆proportion_test_one_sample successes n p
⍝ Z←dm∆proportion_test_one_sample successes n p alternative
⍝ Z←dm∆proportion_test_one_sample successes n p alternative correct
⍝ Z←dm∆proportion_test_one_sample successes n p alternative correct conf_level
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `successes` integer: Integer successes in [0,n].
⍝ - `n` integer: Positive independent-trial count.
⍝ - `p` number, default 0.5: Finite null probability strictly in (0,1), default .5.
⍝ - `alternative` integer (dm∆Alternative∆…, or the enumerator's name), default dm∆Alternative∆TwoSided: TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for true probability relative to p.
⍝ - `correct` boolean (0 or 1), default 1: True (default) applies the capped half-count correction to z.
⍝ - `conf_level` number, default 0.94999999999999996: Finite confidence level in (0,1), default .95.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: statistic=z^2, parameter1=1, estimate1=successes/n, p_value and uncorrected Wilson confidence bounds.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Invalid counts, null probability, alternative or confidence level. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::proportion_test_one_sample(std::size_t successes, std::size_t n, double p = 0.5, Alternative alternative = TwoSided, bool correct = true, double conf_level = 0.94999999999999996)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(198 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆proportion_test_two_sample B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Pooled score test for two proportions, with an unpooled difference interval.
⍝
⍝ Tests equality using the pooled null SE and optionally a capped continuity correction. The
⍝ difference CI uses an unpooled Wald SE and an uncapped half-count width correction when
⍝ requested; it is approximate and is not a Wilson/Newcombe interval. All-zero/all-one pooled
⍝ samples return z=0,p=1. O(1) work/storage, no shared state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto p=dm::stats::proportion_test_two_sample(2,10,3,10);
⍝ std::print("{:.1f}\n",p.estimate1); // 0.2
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/prop.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆proportion_test_two_sample successes1 n1 successes2 n2
⍝ Z←dm∆proportion_test_two_sample successes1 n1 successes2 n2 alternative
⍝ Z←dm∆proportion_test_two_sample successes1 n1 successes2 n2 alternative correct
⍝ Z←dm∆proportion_test_two_sample successes1 n1 successes2 n2 alternative correct conf_level
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `successes1` integer: First success count in [0,n1].
⍝ - `n1` integer: Positive first independent-trial count.
⍝ - `successes2` integer: Second success count in [0,n2].
⍝ - `n2` integer: Positive independent second-trial count.
⍝ - `alternative` integer (dm∆Alternative∆…, or the enumerator's name), default dm∆Alternative∆TwoSided: TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for p1-p2.
⍝ - `correct` boolean (0 or 1), default 1: True (default) corrects the score test and adds a half-count CI width.
⍝ - `conf_level` number, default 0.94999999999999996: Finite confidence level in (0,1), default .95.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: statistic=z^2, parameter1=1, estimates=sample proportions, p_value and confidence bounds for p1-p2, clamped to [-1,1].
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Invalid counts, alternative or confidence level. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::proportion_test_two_sample(std::size_t successes1, std::size_t n1, std::size_t successes2, std::size_t n2, Alternative alternative = TwoSided, bool correct = true, double conf_level = 0.94999999999999996)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(199 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆binomial_test B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Exact binomial test with Clopper-Pearson confidence bounds, like R binom.test().
⍝
⍝ One-sided p-values are inclusive tails. Two-sided p-values sum PMFs no larger than the
⍝ observed PMF, using relative comparison tolerance 1e-7. The equal-tailed Clopper-Pearson
⍝ interval has at-least-nominal coverage but need not invert the probability-ordered two-sided
⍝ test. Null endpoints zero/one are supported. Two-sided enumeration costs O(n), intervals use
⍝ bounded beta-CDF bisection; O(1) auxiliary storage, no shared state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto b=dm::stats::binomial_test(9,10);
⍝ std::print("{:.6f}\n",b.p_value); // 0.021484
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/binom.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆binomial_test successes n
⍝ Z←dm∆binomial_test successes n p
⍝ Z←dm∆binomial_test successes n p alternative
⍝ Z←dm∆binomial_test successes n p alternative conf_level
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `successes` integer: Integer observed successes in [0,n].
⍝ - `n` integer: Positive independent Bernoulli trial count.
⍝ - `p` number, default 0.5: Finite null probability in [0,1], default .5.
⍝ - `alternative` integer (dm∆Alternative∆…, or the enumerator's name), default dm∆Alternative∆TwoSided: TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for true success probability.
⍝ - `conf_level` number, default 0.94999999999999996: Finite confidence level in (0,1), default .95.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: statistic=successes, parameter1=n, estimate1=successes/n, p_value and Clopper-Pearson interval; one-sided intervals end at zero/one.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Invalid counts, probability, alternative or confidence level. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::binomial_test(std::size_t successes, std::size_t n, double p = 0.5, Alternative alternative = TwoSided, double conf_level = 0.94999999999999996)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(200 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆t_test_one_sample B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ One-sample Student t-test of a population mean, like R t.test().
⍝
⍝ Independent normal observations give t=(mean-mu)/SE with n-1 df. The CI estimates the
⍝ population mean, not mean-mu. Inputs are copied only for paired differences; all routines
⍝ leave inputs unchanged and use no shared mutable state. O(n) work; no null/nonfinite omission.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto t=dm::stats::t_test_one_sample({1,2,3,4});
⍝ std::print("{:.0f}\n",t.parameter1); // 3
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/t.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆t_test_one_sample x
⍝ Z←dm∆t_test_one_sample x mu
⍝ Z←dm∆t_test_one_sample x mu alternative
⍝ Z←dm∆t_test_one_sample x mu alternative conf_level
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `x` numeric vector: At least two finite observations with positive representable sample variance.
⍝ - `mu` number, default 0.0: Finite null mean, default zero, in observation units.
⍝ - `alternative` integer (dm∆Alternative∆…, or the enumerator's name), default dm∆Alternative∆TwoSided: TwoSided (default), Less or Greater, relative to mu.
⍝ - `conf_level` number, default 0.94999999999999996: Finite confidence level strictly in (0,1), default .95.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: statistic=t, parameter1=df, estimate1=sample mean, p_value and mean confidence bounds; remaining scalars retain defaults. One-sided intervals have one infinite endpoint.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Too few/nonfinite values, invalid mu/alternative/confidence or zero/nonfinite standard error. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::t_test_one_sample(const std::vector<double> & x, double mu = 0.0, Alternative alternative = TwoSided, double conf_level = 0.94999999999999996)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(201 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆t_test_two_sample B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Independent two-sample Welch or pooled Student mean test, like R t.test().
⍝
⍝ Welch uses unequal sample variances and Satterthwaite approximate df; normality alone does not
⍝ make its reference exact. Pooled Student requires equal population variances and independent
⍝ normal observations for its exact reference. CI estimates mean(x)-mean(y). O(n_x+n_y) work,
⍝ inputs unchanged, no shared mutable state or omission.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto t=dm::stats::t_test_two_sample({1,2,3},{4,5,6});
⍝ std::print("{:.0f}\n",t.estimate1); // 2
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/t.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆t_test_two_sample x y
⍝ Z←dm∆t_test_two_sample x y equal_variance
⍝ Z←dm∆t_test_two_sample x y equal_variance alternative
⍝ Z←dm∆t_test_two_sample x y equal_variance alternative conf_level
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `x` numeric vector: At least two finite observations in the first group.
⍝ - `y` numeric vector: At least two finite independent observations in the second group.
⍝ - `equal_variance` boolean (0 or 1), default 0: False (default) uses Welch; true uses pooled variance.
⍝ - `alternative` integer (dm∆Alternative∆…, or the enumerator's name), default dm∆Alternative∆TwoSided: TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for mean(x)-mean(y).
⍝ - `conf_level` number, default 0.94999999999999996: Finite confidence level in (0,1), default .95.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: statistic=t, parameter1=df, estimate1/2=group means, p_value and confidence bounds for their difference. One-sided bounds can be infinite.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Invalid sample sizes/values, alternative/confidence, unrepresentable mean difference or zero/nonfinite combined standard error. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::t_test_two_sample(const std::vector<double> & x, const std::vector<double> & y, bool equal_variance = false, Alternative alternative = TwoSided, double conf_level = 0.94999999999999996)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(202 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆t_test_paired B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Paired Student t-test on aligned differences, like R t.test(paired=TRUE).
⍝
⍝ Runs the one-sample test on x[i]-y[i]. Differences, rather than the two marginal samples, need
⍝ independent normal sampling for the exact t reference. Row order defines pairing; no values
⍝ are omitted. O(n) work/storage; inputs unchanged, no shared mutable state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto t=dm::stats::t_test_paired({2,4,7},{1,2,4});
⍝ std::print("{:.0f}\n",t.parameter1); // 2
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/t.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆t_test_paired x y
⍝ Z←dm∆t_test_paired x y alternative
⍝ Z←dm∆t_test_paired x y alternative conf_level
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `x` numeric vector: At least two finite first measurements.
⍝ - `y` numeric vector: Equal-length finite second measurements paired by index.
⍝ - `alternative` integer (dm∆Alternative∆…, or the enumerator's name), default dm∆Alternative∆TwoSided: TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for the population mean difference.
⍝ - `conf_level` number, default 0.94999999999999996: Finite confidence level in (0,1), default .95.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: One-sample difference result with method Paired t-test; estimate1 is mean difference, CI targets the same difference.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Length mismatch, nonfinite data/differences, invalid controls or zero/nonfinite difference standard error. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::t_test_paired(const std::vector<double> & x, const std::vector<double> & y, Alternative alternative = TwoSided, double conf_level = 0.94999999999999996)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(203 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆f_test_variance B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ F-test and interval for a ratio of variances, like R var.test().
⍝
⍝ Uses sample variance(x)/sample variance(y). Independent normal populations give the
⍝ F(df_x,df_y) reference; it is sensitive to nonnormality and is not a gatekeeper for choosing
⍝ Welch versus pooled t tests. O(n_x+n_y) work/O(1) auxiliary storage; inputs unchanged, no
⍝ omission/shared state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto f=dm::stats::f_test_variance({1,2,3},{2,4,6});
⍝ std::print("{:.2f}\n",f.estimate1); // 0.25
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/var.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆f_test_variance x y
⍝ Z←dm∆f_test_variance x y alternative
⍝ Z←dm∆f_test_variance x y alternative conf_level
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `x` numeric vector: At least two finite first-sample values with positive finite sample variance.
⍝ - `y` numeric vector: At least two finite independent second-sample values with positive finite sample variance.
⍝ - `alternative` integer (dm∆Alternative∆…, or the enumerator's name), default dm∆Alternative∆TwoSided: TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for population variance ratio relative to one.
⍝ - `conf_level` number, default 0.94999999999999996: Finite confidence level in (0,1), default .95.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: statistic=estimate1=variance ratio, parameter1/2=sample sizes minus one, p_value and ratio confidence bounds; one-sided interval ends at zero or infinity.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Too few/nonfinite observations, nonpositive/nonfinite variances or invalid controls. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::f_test_variance(const std::vector<double> & x, const std::vector<double> & y, Alternative alternative = TwoSided, double conf_level = 0.94999999999999996)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(204 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆wilcoxon_signed_rank_test B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Normal-approximation Wilcoxon signed-rank test with tie/continuity corrections.
⍝
⍝ Tests symmetry of x-mu about zero under independent observations, not an arbitrary-population
⍝ mean or median null. For paired data pass aligned differences. Exact-zero differences are
⍝ removed; absolute differences receive average ranks. Always uses a normal approximation, even
⍝ for small tie-free samples; no exact option or CI. O(n log n) work/O(n) storage; input
⍝ unchanged, no shared state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto w=dm::stats::wilcoxon_signed_rank_test({1,2,3,4});
⍝ std::print("{:.0f}\n",w.statistic); // 10
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/wilcox.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆wilcoxon_signed_rank_test x
⍝ Z←dm∆wilcoxon_signed_rank_test x mu
⍝ Z←dm∆wilcoxon_signed_rank_test x mu alternative
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `x` numeric vector: Finite values; at least one nonzero difference from mu must remain.
⍝ - `mu` number, default 0.0: Finite symmetry centre, default zero.
⍝ - `alternative` integer (dm∆Alternative∆…, or the enumerator's name), default dm∆Alternative∆TwoSided: TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for the signed location shift.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: statistic=positive rank sum V, estimate1=mu (not an estimated pseudomedian), approximate p_value; no CI.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Nonfinite input/mu, invalid alternative, no nonzero differences or zero rank variance. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::wilcoxon_signed_rank_test(const std::vector<double> & x, double mu = 0.0, Alternative alternative = TwoSided)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(205 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆wilcoxon_rank_sum_test B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Tie/continuity-corrected Mann-Whitney U test using a normal approximation.
⍝
⍝ Pools ranks, giving average ranks to ties, and reports U for the first sample. Its identical-
⍝ distribution null requires independent exchangeable samples. A common-shape shift model
⍝ supports a location interpretation; equal medians alone is insufficient. U/(n_x*n_y) estimates
⍝ P(X>Y)+P(X=Y)/2, not a mean difference. Approximation is used at every size. O(N log N)
⍝ work/O(N) storage, inputs unchanged, no shared state.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto w=dm::stats::wilcoxon_rank_sum_test({1,2,3},{4,5,6});
⍝ std::print("{:.0f}\n",w.statistic); // 0
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/wilcox.test.html
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆wilcoxon_rank_sum_test x y
⍝ Z←dm∆wilcoxon_rank_sum_test x y alternative
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `x` numeric vector: Nonempty finite first sample.
⍝ - `y` numeric vector: Nonempty finite independent second sample.
⍝ - `alternative` integer (dm∆Alternative∆…, or the enumerator's name), default dm∆Alternative∆TwoSided: TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for first-versus-second stochastic location.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** HypothesisTestResult handle: statistic=U=rank_sum(x)-n_x(n_x+1)/2 and approximate p_value; no CI or location estimate.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: Empty/nonfinite samples, invalid alternative or zero rank variance (all pooled observations tied). (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::wilcoxon_rank_sum_test(const std::vector<double> & x, const std::vector<double> & y, Alternative alternative = TwoSided)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆HypothesisTestResult`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(206 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆bs B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Construct an owned B-spline basis for repeated evaluation with eval(). Free-function spelling of Bspline: construction fixes the knots and boundaries without evaluating a design matrix or fitting a model. The returned value owns its basis; the input knot vector may be modified or destroyed after this call.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ const auto basis = dm::bs(0, 1, dm::seql(0, 1, 3));
⍝ const auto design = basis.eval(dm::c(0, 0.5, 1));
⍝ std::print("{} {}\n", design.rows(), design.cols()); // 3 4
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆bs lower upper knots
⍝ Z←dm∆bs lower upper knots degree
⍝ Z←dm∆bs lower upper knots degree intercept
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `lower` number: Finite left boundary, strictly below upper.
⍝ - `upper` number: Finite right boundary.
⍝ - `knots` numeric vector: Finite, strictly increasing breakpoints in [lower, upper]. Boundary entries are optional; an empty vector creates a basis without interior knots.
⍝ - `degree` integer, default 3: Polynomial degree, default 3 (cubic).
⍝ - `intercept` boolean (0 or 1), default 0: Keep the first basis column, default false. Leave false with an intercept formula such as `y ~ .`; use a no-intercept formula when true.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** Bspline handle: Owned Bspline with the same degree, intercept policy and validation as its constructor. Call eval() on training and prediction coordinates.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: For invalid boundaries, knots or overflowing degree. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `Bspline datamunge::bs(double lower, double upper, const std::vector<double> & knots, std::size_t degree = 3, bool intercept = false)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆Bspline`, `dm∆eval()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(207 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆mkdir B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Create a directory and any missing parent directories. Accepts native filesystem paths, strings and string literals. An existing directory is a successful no-op; an existing file at the target or along its parents is an error. Relative paths use the current working directory, which this function does not change. Permissions follow the native filesystem and process defaults. A failed recursive operation may leave created parents. This C++ convenience forwards to std::filesystem::create_directories().
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ dm::mkdir("build/figs");
⍝ std::print("{}\n", std::filesystem::is_directory("build/figs")); // true
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆mkdir path
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `path` character vector (path): Directory to create, including any missing parents; must not be empty.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** boolean (0 or 1): True if the target directory was created, false if it already existed.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: native: …`: For an empty path, a conflicting file, insufficient permissions or other operating-system filesystem errors. (C++ `std::filesystem::filesystem_error`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `bool datamunge::mkdir(const std::filesystem::path & path)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆create_directories()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(208 0) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

