∇Z←dm∆refine_basis B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Inserts knots into a clamped B-spline basis (Oslo algorithm).
⍝
⍝ Builds the refined knot vector (old knots merged with `inserted_knots`) and the sparse
⍝ discrete-B-spline operator that re-expresses every coarse spline on it exactly. Each
⍝ row has at most degree + 1 nonzeros and the work is O(m p^2) for m refined functions.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ auto coarse = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(1, 2);   // knots 0 0 1 1
⍝ auto r = dm::fda::refine_basis(coarse, {0.25});
⍝ std::print("{}\n", r.apply({0.0, 4.0}));                   // [0, 1, 4]
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - E. Cohen, T. Lyche and R. Riesenfeld (1980). Discrete B-splines and subdivision
⍝   techniques in computer-aided geometric design and computer graphics. Computer Graphics
⍝   and Image Processing 14(2), 87-111. https://doi.org/10.1016/0146-664X(80)90040-4
⍝ - W. Boehm (1980). Inserting new knots into B-spline curves. Computer-Aided Design
⍝   12(4), 199-201. https://doi.org/10.1016/0010-4485(80)90154-2
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆refine_basis basis inserted_knots
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `basis` BSplineBasis handle: Basis to refine; its knot vector must be clamped (first and last knots repeated degree + 1 times, as produced by BSplineBasis::open_uniform()).
⍝ - `inserted_knots` numeric vector: Knots to add, strictly inside the domain, in any order. Repeats are allowed while every knot multiplicity stays <= degree + 1.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** BSplineRefinement handle: The refined basis and its refinement operator.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: If the basis is not clamped, a knot is outside the open domain or non-finite, or a multiplicity would exceed degree + 1. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `BSplineRefinement datamunge::fda::refine_basis(const BSplineBasis & basis, const std::vector<double> & inserted_knots)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆dyadic_refine()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(234 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆dyadic_refine B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Halves every knot span of a clamped basis (inserts each positive span's midpoint).
⍝
⍝ This is the nested-space step between consecutive levels of HB/THB splines: the refined
⍝ space contains the coarse one, and the operator maps coefficients exactly.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ auto r = dm::fda::dyadic_refine(dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(3, 5));
⍝ std::print("{} -> {}\n", 5, r.basis.basis_size()); // 5 -> 7
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - E. Cohen, T. Lyche and R. Riesenfeld (1980). Discrete B-splines and subdivision
⍝   techniques in computer-aided geometric design and computer graphics.
⍝   https://doi.org/10.1016/0146-664X(80)90040-4
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆dyadic_refine basis
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `basis` BSplineBasis handle: Clamped basis to refine.
⍝
⍝ **Result:** BSplineRefinement handle: The refined basis and its refinement operator.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: If the basis is not clamped. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `BSplineRefinement datamunge::fda::dyadic_refine(const BSplineBasis & basis)`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆refine_basis()`, `dm∆dyadic_refine()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(235 0) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆fit_tensor_bspline B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Fits a tensor-product B-spline to scattered data by ordinary least squares.
⍝
⍝ Builds the sparse design B (each row has at most prod(degree_k + 1) nonzeros), then
⍝ solves (B^T B) c = B^T y. Coordinates outside a basis domain are clamped to it.
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(1, 2);
⍝ auto f = dm::fda::fit_tensor_bspline({b}, {{0.0}, {0.5}, {1.0}}, {1.0, 2.0, 3.0});
⍝ std::print("{:.3f}\n", f.evaluate(0.25)); // 1.500
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - C. de Boor (2001). A Practical Guide to Splines, revised edition. Springer.
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆fit_tensor_bspline bases points values
⍝ Z←dm∆fit_tensor_bspline bases points values options
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `bases` vector of (BSplineBasis handle): One basis per dimension.
⍝ - `points` numeric matrix (one row per element): Sample locations, each of length bases.size().
⍝ - `values` numeric vector: One response per point.
⍝ - `options` SplineSolveOptions handle, default the C++ default: Solver settings (default: automatic).
⍝
⍝ **Result:** TensorBSpline handle: The fitted spline.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: On empty or mismatched inputs, or non-finite data. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: native: …`: If the normal equations are singular (some basis function has too little data; use a penalized fit or fewer functions). (C++ `std::runtime_error`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `TensorBSpline datamunge::fda::fit_tensor_bspline(std::vector<BSplineBasis> bases, const std::vector<std::vector<double>> & points, const std::vector<double> & values, const SplineSolveOptions & options = {})`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆fit_tensor_pspline()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(236 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
∇

∇Z←dm∆fit_tensor_pspline B;⍙M;⍙E
⍝ Fits a tensor-product P-spline: least squares plus an anisotropic difference penalty, solving (B^T B + P) c = B^T y with P from tensor_difference_penalty().
⍝
⍝ All-zero lambdas reproduce fit_tensor_bspline(). Positive lambdas keep the system
⍝ well-posed even with fewer points than coefficients (for order 0, or once the data
⍝ determine the order-q polynomial null space).
⍝
⍝ **Example**
⍝ ```
⍝ auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(3, 8);
⍝ auto f = dm::fda::fit_tensor_pspline({b}, {{0.0}, {1.0}}, {0.0, 1.0}, {1e3});
⍝ std::print("{:.3f}\n", f.evaluate(0.5)); // 0.500
⍝ ```
⍝
⍝ **References**
⍝ - P. H. C. Eilers and B. D. Marx (1996). Flexible smoothing with B-splines and penalties.
⍝   https://doi.org/10.1214/ss/1038425655
⍝ - P. H. C. Eilers, I. D. Currie and M. Durban (2006). Fast and compact smoothing on large
⍝   multidimensional grids. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.csda.2004.07.008
⍝
⍝ **Usage:**
⍝ ```apl
⍝ Z←dm∆fit_tensor_pspline bases points values lambdas
⍝ Z←dm∆fit_tensor_pspline bases points values lambdas order
⍝ Z←dm∆fit_tensor_pspline bases points values lambdas order options
⍝ ```
⍝ The right argument is the vector of arguments (one argument may be given alone; ⍬ for none).
⍝
⍝ **Arguments:**
⍝ - `bases` vector of (BSplineBasis handle): One basis per dimension.
⍝ - `points` numeric matrix (one row per element): Sample locations, each of length bases.size().
⍝ - `values` numeric vector: One response per point.
⍝ - `lambdas` numeric vector: Nonnegative per-axis smoothing weights, one per basis.
⍝ - `order` integer, default 2: Difference order (default 2, the conventional curvature penalty).
⍝ - `options` SplineSolveOptions handle, default the C++ default: Solver settings (default: automatic).
⍝
⍝ **Result:** TensorBSpline handle: The fitted spline.
⍝
⍝ **Errors:**
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: invalid_argument: …`: On empty/mismatched inputs, invalid lambdas, or an axis with no more functions than `order`. (C++ `std::invalid_argument`)
⍝ - DOMAIN ERROR `dm: native: …`: If the penalized normal equations are singular. (C++ `std::runtime_error`)
⍝
⍝ - C++: `TensorBSpline datamunge::fda::fit_tensor_pspline(std::vector<BSplineBasis> bases, const std::vector<std::vector<double>> & points, const std::vector<double> & values, const std::vector<double> & lambdas, int order = 2, const SplineSolveOptions & options = {})`
⍝
⍝ **See also:** `dm∆fit_tensor_bspline()`, `dm∆fit_pspline()`
 (⍙M ⍙E Z)←(237 1) dm⍙native B
 ⍙M ⎕ES ⍙E
